{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AFPzoTRkFipy2kjtDXJK8z","type":"tool_result","content":"1\ttier: median\n2\tnarrative_guidance: 'Soften \"systematic gradient\" to \"benchmark-conditional variance\n3\t  structure\" or \"task-type clustering.\" Cross-benchmark pattern is 2-cluster ({MMLU,\n4\t  ARC} high item dominance vs {GSM8K, HellaSwag} lower), not a clean monotonic 4-point\n5\t  gradient. Item dominance remains dominant on knowledge benchmarks but magnitude\n6\t  is less precise than MVP suggested once model-as-facet is handled and bootstrap\n7\t  CIs computed. Separability holds on knowledge benchmarks but GSM8K shows infra×method\n8\t  ~3% under binary scoring, requiring benchmark+metric qualification on Claim 2. Bootstrap\n9\t  CIs on gradient endpoints overlap partially — directional claims hold, magnitude\n10\t  claims are imprecise. Reframe hook: \"the variance structure itself depends on what\n11\t  you''re benchmarking\" rather than a clean ordering. FP32 degradation is real (not\n12\t  engine confound), position as known finding (Yuan 2025) with novel item-level G-theory\n13\t  breakdown. Model-as-facet absorption (21% on MMLU) reframes prior single-model studies\n14\t  as having overestimated pure item dominance — turn this into a feature, not a weakness.\n15\t  Target AAAI with adjusted framing; if exp-008 (MATH) is feasible and strengthens\n16\t  the clustering to 3-tier, include it; otherwise ACL ARR October is a comfortable\n17\t  fallback.\n18\t\n19\t  '\n20\timagined_experiments:\n21\t- name: Full-scale MMLU precision study (confound-fixed)\n22\t  plan_id: exp-001\n23\t  description: '4 models × 3 precisions (FP32/BF16/FP16, all V0 enforce_eager) × 6\n24\t    prompts × 6 seeds × 3 temperatures × 4 orderings × 200 MMLU items (stratified).\n25\t    1,036,800 total observations.\n26\t\n27\t    '\n28\t  expected_results:\n29\t    total_observations: 1036800\n30\t    total_conditions: 5184\n31\t    accuracy_fp32: 0.655\n32\t    accuracy_bf16: 0.684\n33\t    accuracy_fp16: 0.681\n34\t    accuracy_gap_fp32_bf16_pp: 2.9\n35\t    accuracy_gap_bf16_fp16_pp: 0.3\n36\t    accuracy_by_model:\n37\t      qwen3_8b:\n38\t        fp32: 0.695\n39\t        bf16: 0.71\n40\t        fp16: 0.706\n41\t      llama31_8b:\n42\t        fp32: 0.6\n43\t        bf16: 0.672\n44\t        fp16: 0.669\n45\t      gemma2_9b:\n46\t        fp32: 0.638\n47\t        bf16: 0.66\n48\t        fp16: 0.658\n49\t      mistral_7b:\n50\t        fp32: 0.688\n51\t        bf16: 0.694\n52\t        fp16: 0.691\n53\t    item_id_variance_binary: 0.52\n54\t    residual_variance_binary: 0.14\n55\t    precision_x_item_binary: 0.05\n56\t    prompt_x_item_binary: 0.058\n57\t    temperature_x_item_binary: 0.055\n58\t    seed_x_item_binary: 0.028\n59\t    precision_main_binary: 0.03\n60\t    temperature_main_binary: 0.015\n61\t    prompt_main_binary: 0.008\n62\t    ordering_main_binary: 0.002\n63\t    precision_x_temperature_binary: 0.002\n64\t    precision_x_prompt_binary: 0.001\n65\t    precision_x_seed_binary: 0.001\n66\t    other_binary: 0.09\n67\t    residual_textmatch: 0.36\n68\t    prompt_x_item_textmatch: 0.33\n69\t    temperature_main_textmatch: 0.14\n70\t    precision_x_item_textmatch: 0.04\n71\t    precision_x_temperature_textmatch: 0.022\n72\t    temperature_x_item_textmatch: 0.03\n73\t    item_id_textmatch: 0.03\n74\t    precision_main_textmatch: 0.025\n75\t    other_textmatch: 0.023\n76\t    g_correct_binary: 0.37\n77\t    g_correct_textmatch: 0.97\n78\t    item_id_rank_corr_across_models: 0.82\n79\t    fp32_gap_model_range_pp:\n80\t    - 1.5\n81\t    - 7.2\n82\t  result_rationale: 'V0 enforce_eager for all precisions eliminates the 0.22pp engine\n83\t    confound (bf16_eager_disentangle) but the FP32 accuracy gap PERSISTS at ~2.9pp\n84\t    average — confirming the disentangle conclusion that 97.4% of the gap was genuine\n85\t    precision effect. FP32 degradation is strongly model-dependent: Llama-3.1-8B shows\n86\t    7.2pp gap (consistent with MVP''s 8.5pp on this model) while Qwen3-8B shows only\n87\t    1.5pp, likely because Qwen''s training used mixed-precision strategies that are\n88\t    more robust to FP32 inference rounding. Precision main effect at 3% is real but\n89\t    dwarfed by item_id at 52%. Precision:item at 5% means certain items (those near\n90\t    decision boundaries) are disproportionately affected — this is the novel item-level\n91\t    breakdown that extends Yuan et al. (2025). Item_id at 52% is lower than MVP''s\n92\t    58.4% because 200 stratified items (vs 50) include more mid-difficulty items that\n93\t    reduce between-item variance, and pooling across 4 models absorbs some item-specific\n94\t    variance into residual. Residual at 14% is inflated because model effects are\n95\t    pooled — exp-004 will decompose this. G=0.37 replicates MVP''s 0.357, confirming\n96\t    item dominance makes G≥0.90 unreachable without massive item expansion. Text-match\n97\t    scoring replicates the qualitative shift: prompt:item dominates (33%), precision×temperature\n98\t    at 2.2% (consistent with MVP''s 1.3% but larger due to more conditions), driven\n99\t    by floating-point non-associativity in generation.\n100\t\n101\t    '\n102\t  tables:\n103\t  - name: 'Table 1: MMLU Variance Decomposition — Binary Correct (Basic, Model Pooled)'\n104\t    columns:\n105\t    - Source\n106\t    - '% Total Variance'\n107\t    rows:\n108\t    - - item_id\n109\t      - 52.0%\n110\t    - - residual (includes model effects)\n111\t      - 14.0%\n112\t    - - prompt:item\n113\t      - 5.8%\n114\t    - - temperature:item\n115\t      - 5.5%\n116\t    - - precision:item\n117\t      - 5.0%\n118\t    - - precision (main)\n119\t      - 3.0%\n120\t    - - seed:item\n121\t      - 2.8%\n122\t    - - temperature (main)\n123\t      - 1.5%\n124\t    - - prompt (main)\n125\t      - 0.8%\n126\t    - - infra×method (all)\n127\t      - 0.4%\n128\t    - - ordering\n129\t      - 0.2%\n130\t    - - other 2-way / 3-way\n131\t      - 9.0%\n132\t  - name: 'Table 2: MMLU Variance Decomposition — Text Exact Match (Basic)'\n133\t    columns:\n134\t    - Source\n135\t    - '% Total Variance'\n136\t    rows:\n137\t    - - residual\n138\t      - 36.0%\n139\t    - - prompt:item\n140\t      - 33.0%\n141\t    - - temperature (main)\n142\t      - 14.0%\n143\t    - - precision:item\n144\t      - 4.0%\n145\t    - - temperature:item\n146\t      - 3.0%\n147\t    - - item_id\n148\t      - 3.0%\n149\t    - - precision (main)\n150\t      - 2.5%\n151\t    - - precision×temperature\n152\t      - 2.2%\n153\t    - - other\n154\t      - 2.3%\n155\t  - name: 'Table 3: Per-Model Accuracy by Precision (V0 enforce_eager)'\n156\t    columns:\n157\t    - Model\n158\t    - FP32\n159\t    - BF16\n160\t    - FP16\n161\t    - FP32-BF16 Gap\n162\t    rows:\n163\t    - - Qwen3-8B-Instruct\n164\t      - '0.695'\n165\t      - '0.710'\n166\t      - '0.706'\n167\t      - 1.5pp\n168\t    - - Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct\n169\t      - '0.600'\n170\t      - '0.672'\n171\t      - '0.669'\n172\t      - 7.2pp\n173\t    - - Gemma-2-9B-IT\n174\t      - '0.638'\n175\t      - '0.660'\n176\t      - '0.658'\n177\t      - 2.2pp\n178\t    - - Mistral-7B-v0.3\n179\t      - '0.688'\n180\t      - '0.694'\n181\t      - '0.691'\n182\t      - 0.6pp\n183\t    - - Average\n184\t      - '0.655'\n185\t      - '0.684'\n186\t      - '0.681'\n187\t      - 2.9pp\n188\t  figures_description:\n189\t  - 'Stacked bar: binary vs text-match variance decomposition side by side, showing\n190\t    metric-conditional shift from item-dominated (binary) to prompt-dominated (text-match).'\n191\t  - 'Heatmap: FP32 degradation (pp) by model × item difficulty quartile, showing model-dependent\n192\t    and difficulty-dependent precision sensitivity.'\n193\t  - 'Scatter: item-level accuracy variance vs item difficulty, colored by MMLU subject\n194\t    category.'\n195\t  analysis: 'Key finding: FP32 degradation is REAL, not an engine confound. The bf16_eager_disentangle\n196\t    result is confirmed at scale — V0 enforce_eager changes nothing substantial. This\n197\t    reframes the contribution: we provide the first item-level G-theory decomposition\n198\t    of precision effects, showing that precision:item (5%) exceeds precision main\n199\t    (3%). The model-dependence of FP32 degradation (1.5-7.2pp) is novel and suggests\n200\t    training-time precision choices affect inference robustness. For the paper: position\n201\t    FP32 degradation as supporting Yuan et al. (2025) with novel item×model granularity,\n202\t    NOT as a primary finding. Separability on MMLU is confirmed: infra×method = 0.4%\n203\t    binary, 2.2% text-match (precision×temperature only). CIs deferred to exp-004.\n204\t\n205\t    '\n206\t- name: Item resampling stability test\n207\t  plan_id: exp-001b\n208\t  description: '3 independent stratified samples of 200 MMLU items (seeds 0, 7, 99)\n209\t    × Llama-3.1-8B × 3 precisions × 3 prompts × 3 seeds × 2 orderings = 54 conditions/sample,\n210\t    32,400 obs/sample.\n211\t\n212\t    '\n213\t  expected_results:\n214\t    item_id_sample_0: 0.49\n215\t    item_id_sample_7: 0.55\n216\t    item_id_sample_99: 0.44\n217\t    item_id_mean: 0.493\n218\t    item_id_sd: 0.055\n219\t    item_id_cv: 0.19\n220\t    g_coeff_sample_0: 0.36\n221\t    g_coeff_sample_7: 0.32\n222\t    g_coeff_sample_99: 0.39\n223\t    accuracy_sample_0: 0.668\n224\t    accuracy_sample_7: 0.641\n225\t    accuracy_sample_99: 0.679\n226\t    mean_item_difficulty_sample_0: 0.5\n227\t    mean_item_difficulty_sample_7: 0.42\n228\t    mean_item_difficulty_sample_99: 0.54\n229\t    sd_item_difficulty_sample_0: 0.28\n230\t    sd_item_difficulty_sample_7: 0.32\n231\t    sd_item_difficulty_sample_99: 0.25\n232\t    rank_order_top3_sources_preserved: true\n233\t    residual_cv: 0.11\n234\t    prompt_item_cv: 0.09\n235\t  result_rationale: 'CV of 19% reflects meaningful item-sampling uncertainty. MMLU\n236\t    has 14,042 items across 57 subjects with heterogeneous difficulty; stratified\n237\t    sampling with 200 items (1.4%) leaves substantial sampling variance especially\n238\t    in the tails. Sample 7 draws more hard items (mean difficulty 0.42), inflating\n239\t    between-item variance to 55% because the difficulty spread is wider (SD 0.32).\n240\t    Sample 99 draws more mid-difficulty items (mean 0.54, SD 0.25), compressing item_id\n241\t    to 44% because items cluster around the center. Despite magnitude variation, the\n242\t    qualitative hierarchy is preserved: item_id ranks #1 in all 3 samples, residual\n243\t    #2, prompt:item #3. This stability-in-rank with instability-in-magnitude is the\n244\t    key finding: the headline claim \"item dominance ~50%\" needs error bars, reported\n245\t    as \"44-55% across independent 200-item stratified draws.\" The G-coefficient varies\n246\t    inversely (0.32-0.39) because higher item variance = lower generalizability of\n247\t    the benchmark score. This directly motivates D-study item-count recommendations\n248\t    with CIs, not point estimates — if your headline variance component shifts ±5pp\n249\t    with a different item draw, your optimal resource allocation shifts too.\n250\t\n251\t    '\n252\t  tables:\n253\t  - name: 'Table 4: Resampling Stability of Variance Components'\n254\t    columns:\n255\t    - Source\n256\t    - Sample 0\n257\t    - Sample 7\n258\t    - Sample 99\n259\t    - Mean\n260\t    - CV\n261\t    rows:\n262\t    - - item_id\n263\t      - 49.0%\n264\t      - 55.0%\n265\t      - 44.0%\n266\t      - 49.3%\n267\t      - '0.19'\n268\t    - - residual\n269\t      - 17.5%\n270\t      - 15.0%\n271\t      - 19.5%\n272\t      - 17.3%\n273\t      - '0.11'\n274\t    - - prompt:item\n275\t      - 6.5%\n276\t      - 6.0%\n277\t      - 7.0%\n278\t      - 6.5%\n279\t      - '0.09'\n280\t    - - temp:item\n281\t      - 5.8%\n282\t      - 5.2%\n283\t      - 6.5%\n284\t      - 5.8%\n285\t      - '0.10'\n286\t    - - prec:item\n287\t      - 5.0%\n288\t      - 5.5%\n289\t      - 4.2%\n290\t      - 4.9%\n291\t      - '0.12'\n292\t    - - precision\n293\t      - 3.0%\n294\t      - 3.5%\n295\t      - 2.8%\n296\t      - 3.1%\n297\t      - '0.10'\n298\t    - - other\n299\t      - 13.2%\n300\t      - 9.8%\n301\t      - 16.0%\n302\t      - 13.0%\n303\t      - —\n304\t  - name: 'Table 5: Per-Sample Summary Statistics'\n305\t    columns:\n306\t    - Metric\n307\t    - Sample 0\n308\t    - Sample 7\n309\t    - Sample 99\n310\t    rows:\n311\t    - - N items\n312\t      - '200'\n313\t      - '200'\n314\t      - '200'\n315\t    - - Mean item difficulty\n316\t      - '0.50'\n317\t      - '0.42'\n318\t      - '0.54'\n319\t    - - SD item difficulty\n320\t      - '0.28'\n321\t      - '0.32'\n322\t      - '0.25'\n323\t    - - Overall accuracy\n324\t      - '0.668'\n325\t      - '0.641'\n326\t      - '0.679'\n327\t    - - G (binary)\n328\t      - '0.36'\n329\t      - '0.32'\n330\t      - '0.39'\n331\t    - - item_id %\n332\t      - 49.0%\n333\t      - 55.0%\n334\t      - 44.0%\n335\t  figures_description:\n336\t  - 'Forest plot: item_id % with bootstrap 95% CIs across 3 resamples, showing overlapping\n337\t    but distinct intervals. Horizontal shaded band marks the MVP point estimate (58.4%)\n338\t    for comparison.'\n339\t  - 'Histogram overlay: item difficulty distribution for each sample, showing how\n340\t    stratification seed shifts the difficulty profile despite identical stratification\n341\t    ratios.'\n342\t  analysis: 'The 19% CV is the paper''s best defense against \"you just got lucky with\n343\t    your items.\" It quantifies the item-sampling uncertainty that plagues every single-draw\n344\t    benchmark study. Rank ordering is robust (item_id always #1) but precise magnitude\n345\t    depends on which items you drew — explaining why Mesting (2026) and Brittlebench\n346\t    (2026) reached different headline numbers on different item sets. For the paper:\n347\t    always report item_id as a range (44-55%) not a point. D-study projections from\n348\t    exp-004 should include CIs that account for this resampling instability.\n349\t\n350\t    '\n351\t- name: Multi-benchmark replication (GSM8K, ARC, HellaSwag)\n352\t  plan_id: exp-002\n353\t  description: '4 models × 2 precisions (BF16/FP16) × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 2 temperatures\n354\t    × 4 orderings × 200 items on GSM8K, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag. Reduced crossing\n355\t    vs exp-001.\n356\t\n357\t    '\n358\t  expected_results:\n359\t    total_observations_per_benchmark: 384000\n360\t    gsm8k_accuracy: 0.575\n361\t    gsm8k_item_id_binary: 0.4\n362\t    gsm8k_residual_binary: 0.15\n363\t    gsm8k_temp_x_item_binary: 0.1\n364\t    gsm8k_seed_x_item_binary: 0.07\n365\t    gsm8k_prompt_x_item_binary: 0.065\n366\t    gsm8k_temperature_binary: 0.04\n367\t    gsm8k_prec_x_temp_binary: 0.022\n368\t    gsm8k_prec_x_seed_binary: 0.005\n369\t    gsm8k_prec_x_prompt_binary: 0.003\n370\t    gsm8k_infra_x_method_total_binary: 0.03\n371\t    gsm8k_precision_main_binary: 0.01\n372\t    gsm8k_prec_x_item_binary: 0.015\n373\t    gsm8k_other_binary: 0.093\n374\t    gsm8k_g_correct_binary: 0.42\n375\t    arc_accuracy: 0.625\n376\t    arc_item_id_binary: 0.5\n377\t    arc_residual_binary: 0.12\n378\t    arc_prompt_x_item_binary: 0.085\n379\t    arc_temp_x_item_binary: 0.055\n380\t    arc_seed_x_item_binary: 0.04\n381\t    arc_prec_x_item_binary: 0.025\n382\t    arc_temperature_binary: 0.025\n383\t    arc_precision_binary: 0.01\n384\t    arc_infra_x_method_total_binary: 0.005\n385\t    arc_other_binary: 0.115\n386\t    arc_g_correct_binary: 0.35\n387\t    hellaswag_accuracy: 0.712\n388\t    hellaswag_item_id_binary: 0.35\n389\t    hellaswag_residual_binary: 0.18\n390\t    hellaswag_temp_x_item_binary: 0.095\n391\t    hellaswag_seed_x_item_binary: 0.085\n392\t    hellaswag_prompt_x_item_binary: 0.075\n393\t    hellaswag_temperature_binary: 0.045\n394\t    hellaswag_prec_x_item_binary: 0.015\n395\t    hellaswag_precision_binary: 0.005\n396\t    hellaswag_infra_x_method_total_binary: 0.003\n397\t    hellaswag_other_binary: 0.127\n398\t    hellaswag_g_correct_binary: 0.48\n399\t    gradient_pattern: '2-cluster: {MMLU 52%, ARC 50%} vs {GSM8K 40%, HellaSwag 35%}'\n400\t    gradient_spread_pp: 17\n401\t    within_cluster_ordering_reliable: false\n402\t    gsm8k_prec_x_temp_textmatch: 0.035\n403\t    arc_prec_x_temp_textmatch: 0.022\n404\t    hellaswag_prec_x_temp_textmatch: 0.016\n405\t    infra_x_method_textmatch_max: 0.035\n406\t  result_rationale: 'The 2-cluster pattern emerges because variance structure tracks\n407\t    cognitive demand, not benchmark labels. Knowledge-retrieval benchmarks (MMLU,\n408\t    ARC) show high item dominance (50-52% basic) because item difficulty is the primary\n409\t    driver — items test factual knowledge that is either present or absent in the\n410\t    model, and this binary state dominates across conditions. ARC clusters with MMLU\n411\t    despite being labeled \"science reasoning\" because most ARC items are effectively\n412\t    factual recall (what happens when ice melts?) rather than multi-step inference.\n413\t    Reasoning benchmarks (GSM8K, HellaSwag) show lower item dominance (35-40%) because\n414\t    temperature and seed interact strongly with items: multi-step reasoning and commonsense\n415\t    inference have multiple valid processing paths, and sampling randomness determines\n416\t    which path is taken, shifting variance from item_id to interaction terms. GSM8K\n417\t    item dominance at 40% is above the hypothesized 20-30% because even math reasoning\n418\t    has trivially easy items (single-step arithmetic) and impossible items (complex\n419\t    multi-step for 8B models), creating a floor of item-driven variance. The critical\n420\t    median-tier finding: GSM8K shows infra×method = 3.0% under binary scoring (precision×temperature\n421\t    2.2% + precision×seed 0.5% + precision×prompt 0.3%). This breaks universal separability.\n422\t    The mechanism: math generation produces longer chains-of-thought where BF16 vs\n423\t    FP16 accumulation differences compound; temperature controls how aggressively\n424\t    alternative reasoning paths are explored; the precision×temperature combination\n425\t    can push probability distributions past decision boundaries, flipping the final\n426\t    numerical answer. Binary scoring captures this because the extracted number differs.\n427\t    Other benchmarks show infra×method <0.5% binary — the effect is GSM8K-specific.\n428\t    Under text-match, GSM8K prec×temp reaches 3.5% because exact-string comparison\n429\t    of multi-step outputs amplifies floating-point divergence. The gradient spread\n430\t    of 17pp (MMLU 52% vs HellaSwag 35%, basic) is within the target range [10-24pp];\n431\t    after model-as-facet correction in exp-004 it will be similar (48% vs 31% = 17pp).\n432\t\n433\t    '\n434\t  tables:\n435\t  - name: 'Table 6: Cross-Benchmark Variance Decomposition — Binary (Basic, Model\n436\t      Pooled)'\n437\t    columns:\n438\t    - Source\n439\t    - MMLU\n440\t    - GSM8K\n441\t    - ARC\n442\t    - HellaSwag\n443\t    rows:\n444\t    - - item_id\n445\t      - 52.0%\n446\t      - 40.0%\n447\t      - 50.0%\n448\t      - 35.0%\n449\t    - - residual\n450\t      - 14.0%\n451\t      - 15.0%\n452\t      - 12.0%\n453\t      - 18.0%\n454\t    - - temp:item\n455\t      - 5.5%\n456\t      - 10.0%\n457\t      - 5.5%\n458\t      - 9.5%\n459\t    - - seed:item\n460\t      - 2.8%\n461\t      - 7.0%\n462\t      - 4.0%\n463\t      - 8.5%\n464\t    - - prompt:item\n465\t      - 5.8%\n466\t      - 6.5%\n467\t      - 8.5%\n468\t      - 7.5%\n469\t    - - prec:item\n470\t      - 5.0%\n471\t      - 1.5%\n472\t      - 2.5%\n473\t      - 1.5%\n474\t    - - precision (main)\n475\t      - 3.0%\n476\t      - 1.0%\n477\t      - 1.0%\n478\t      - 0.5%\n479\t    - - temperature (main)\n480\t      - 1.5%\n481\t      - 4.0%\n482\t      - 2.5%\n483\t      - 4.5%\n484\t    - - infra×method (total)\n485\t      - 0.4%\n486\t      - 3.0%\n487\t      - 0.5%\n488\t      - 0.3%\n489\t    - - other\n490\t      - 10.0%\n491\t      - 12.0%\n492\t      - 13.5%\n493\t      - 14.7%\n494\t  - name: 'Table 7: Cross-Benchmark Summary (Basic)'\n495\t    columns:\n496\t    - Benchmark\n497\t    - Task Type\n498\t    - item_id %\n499\t    - G (binary)\n500\t    - infra×method\n501\t    - Cluster\n502\t    rows:\n503\t    - - MMLU\n504\t      - Knowledge\n505\t      - 52.0%\n506\t      - '0.37'\n507\t      - 0.4%\n508\t      - High\n509\t    - - ARC-Challenge\n510\t      - Knowledge/Science\n511\t      - 50.0%\n512\t      - '0.35'\n513\t      - 0.5%\n514\t      - High\n515\t    - - GSM8K\n516\t      - Math reasoning\n517\t      - 40.0%\n518\t      - '0.42'\n519\t      - 3.0%\n520\t      - Low\n521\t    - - HellaSwag\n522\t      - Commonsense\n523\t      - 35.0%\n524\t      - '0.48'\n525\t      - 0.3%\n526\t      - Low\n527\t  - name: 'Table 8: GSM8K Infra×Method Interaction Breakdown (Binary)'\n528\t    columns:\n529\t    - Interaction\n530\t    - '% Total Variance'\n531\t    - 95% CI (bootstrap, exp-004)\n532\t    rows:\n533\t    - - precision×temperature\n534\t      - 2.2%\n535\t      - deferred\n536\t    - - precision×seed\n537\t      - 0.5%\n538\t      - deferred\n539\t    - - precision×prompt\n540\t      - 0.3%\n541\t      - deferred\n542\t    - - Total infra×method\n543\t      - 3.0%\n544\t      - deferred\n545\t    note: Only GSM8K exceeds 1% threshold. MMLU 0.4%, ARC 0.5%, HellaSwag 0.3%.\n546\t  figures_description:\n547\t  - 'Stacked bar (signature figure): 4-benchmark variance decomposition, bars sorted\n548\t    by item_id descending, showing 2-cluster visual. Item_id bars in dark blue, interaction\n549\t    terms in lighter shades, precision/temperature main in warm tones.'\n550\t  - 'Scatter: item_id % vs benchmark task-type ordinal (knowledge → reasoning), with\n551\t    error bars from exp-001b resampling on MMLU. Shows cluster structure, not monotonic\n552\t    trend.'\n553\t  - 'Heatmap: pairwise Spearman correlation of per-item variance profiles across 4\n554\t    benchmarks, showing r>0.7 within clusters and r<0.3 between clusters.'\n555\t  analysis: 'This is the core finding. The 2-cluster pattern is clear and the direction\n556\t    is robust, but it is NOT the clean monotonic gradient originally hypothesized.\n557\t    ARC clusters with MMLU (not intermediate) — this is actually a stronger finding\n558\t    than a gradient: variance structure tracks cognitive demand, and ARC''s \"science\n559\t    reasoning\" label is misleading. The GSM8K infra×method at 3% binary is the median\n560\t    tier''s key complication for Claim 2: separability is benchmark-conditional, not\n561\t    universal. The paper must add \"on knowledge-retrieval benchmarks\" or \"when math\n562\t    reasoning is excluded\" to the separability claim. Under text-match, GSM8K reaches\n563\t    3.5% (within target [1.5%, 4.5%]). Practical implication: practitioners evaluating\n564\t    math reasoning on GSM8K should fix both precision AND temperature to minimize\n565\t    infra×method confounding.\n566\t\n567\t    '\n568\t- name: Multi-hardware G-study\n569\t  plan_id: exp-003\n570\t  description: '4 models × 2 GPU types (RTX PRO 6000 + second GPU) × 2 precisions\n571\t    (BF16/FP16) × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 3 temperatures × 4 orderings × 200 MMLU items.\n572\t\n573\t    '\n574\t  expected_results:\n575\t    total_observations: 1152000\n576\t    hardware_main_binary: 0.004\n577\t    hardware_x_precision_binary: 0.008\n578\t    hardware_x_temperature_binary: 0.003\n579\t    hardware_x_seed_binary: 0.002\n580\t    hardware_x_prompt_binary: 0.001\n581\t    hardware_x_methodology_total_binary: 0.014\n582\t    hardware_x_item_binary: 0.018\n583\t    total_hardware_related_binary: 0.036\n584\t    hardware_main_textmatch: 0.012\n585\t    hardware_x_precision_textmatch: 0.022\n586\t    hardware_x_methodology_total_textmatch: 0.032\n587\t    hardware_x_item_textmatch: 0.028\n588\t    total_hardware_related_textmatch: 0.072\n589\t    item_id_with_hardware_binary: 0.5\n590\t    g_correct_binary_gpu1: 0.37\n591\t    g_correct_binary_gpu2: 0.36\n592\t    cross_gpu_gap_bf16_mean_pp: 0.12\n593\t    cross_gpu_gap_fp16_mean_pp: 0.75\n594\t    cross_gpu_gap_bf16_max_pp: 0.2\n595\t    cross_gpu_gap_fp16_max_pp: 1.1\n596\t    model_ranking_preserved_across_gpus: true\n597\t    hardware_variance_fp16_subset: 0.022\n598\t    hardware_variance_bf16_subset: 0.003\n599\t  result_rationale: 'Hardware main effect at 0.4% binary is negligible because both\n600\t    GPUs implement the same IEEE 754 operations; differences arise only from CUDA\n601\t    kernel scheduling nondeterminism and memory subsystem timing. The critical finding\n602\t    is hardware×precision at 0.8% binary: FP16 is more hardware-sensitive than BF16\n603\t    (cross-GPU gap 0.75pp vs 0.12pp), because FP16''s narrower dynamic range (5-bit\n604\t    exponent vs BF16''s 8-bit) makes accumulation order hardware-dependent — consistent\n605\t    with Yuan et al. (2025). When subsetting to FP16-only data, hardware explains\n606\t    2.2% of variance (within median narrative''s \"1-3% on one precision\"). Under text-match,\n607\t    hardware effects roughly double (total 7.2%) because different GPU kernel orderings\n608\t    produce different final tokens in long generations, amplified by exact-match scoring.\n609\t    Hardware×item at 1.8% binary means certain items near decision boundaries are\n610\t    hardware-sensitive — the same items that are precision-sensitive (prec:item) are\n611\t    also hardware-sensitive, consistent with a shared mechanism of logit perturbation\n612\t    near thresholds. Model rankings are fully preserved across GPUs (Kendall tau =\n613\t    1.0 at all condition combinations) — hardware effects are far too small to alter\n614\t    comparative model evaluation. Total hardware-related variance at 3.6% binary is\n615\t    manageable but nonzero, requiring \"approximately separable\" language.\n616\t\n617\t    '\n618\t  tables:\n619\t  - name: 'Table 9: Hardware Facet Variance Components'\n620\t    columns:\n621\t    - Source\n622\t    - Binary %\n623\t    - Text-match %\n624\t    rows:\n625\t    - - hardware (main)\n626\t      - 0.4%\n627\t      - 1.2%\n628\t    - - hardware×precision\n629\t      - 0.8%\n630\t      - 2.2%\n631\t    - - hardware×temperature\n632\t      - 0.3%\n633\t      - 0.6%\n634\t    - - hardware×seed\n635\t      - 0.2%\n636\t      - 0.4%\n637\t    - - hardware×prompt\n638\t      - 0.1%\n639\t      - 0.3%\n640\t    - - hardware×item\n641\t      - 1.8%\n642\t      - 2.8%\n643\t    - - Total hardware-related\n644\t      - 3.6%\n645\t      - 7.5%\n646\t  - name: 'Table 10: Cross-GPU Accuracy by Model and Precision'\n647\t    columns:\n648\t    - Model\n649\t    - GPU1-BF16\n650\t    - GPU2-BF16\n651\t    - Δ(pp)\n652\t    - GPU1-FP16\n653\t    - GPU2-FP16\n654\t    - Δ(pp)\n655\t    rows:\n656\t    - - Qwen3-8B\n657\t      - '0.710'\n658\t      - '0.709'\n659\t      - '0.1'\n660\t      - '0.706'\n661\t      - '0.700'\n662\t      - '0.6'\n663\t    - - Llama-3.1-8B\n664\t      - '0.672'\n665\t      - '0.672'\n666\t      - '0.0'\n667\t      - '0.669'\n668\t      - '0.661'\n669\t      - '0.8'\n670\t    - - Gemma-2-9B\n671\t      - '0.660'\n672\t      - '0.659'\n673\t      - '0.1'\n674\t      - '0.658'\n675\t      - '0.649'\n676\t      - '0.9'\n677\t    - - Mistral-7B\n678\t      - '0.694'\n679\t      - '0.693'\n680\t      - '0.1'\n681\t      - '0.691'\n682\t      - '0.680'\n683\t      - '1.1'\n684\t  - name: 'Table 11: Hardware Effect Conditioned on Precision'\n685\t    columns:\n686\t    - Metric\n687\t    - BF16 Only\n688\t    - FP16 Only\n689\t    rows:\n690\t    - - Hardware % total variance\n691\t      - 0.3%\n692\t      - 2.2%\n693\t    - - Mean cross-GPU gap (pp)\n694\t      - '0.12'\n695\t      - '0.75'\n696\t    - - Max cross-GPU gap (pp)\n697\t      - '0.2'\n698\t      - '1.1'\n699\t  figures_description:\n700\t  - 'Paired dot plot: per-model accuracy on GPU1 vs GPU2, BF16 (nearly overlapping)\n701\t    vs FP16 (visible gap), connected by lines to show model-specific hardware sensitivity.'\n702\t  - 'Bar chart: hardware-related variance by scoring metric, showing binary→text-match\n703\t    doubling.'\n704\t  analysis: 'Hardware is a manageable confound, not a showstopper. Separability claim\n705\t    (Claim 2) survives with qualification: \"hardware×methodology is ≤1.4% under binary\n706\t    scoring\" — but the FP16 hardware sensitivity (2.2% when conditioned on FP16) warrants\n707\t    a Discussion paragraph. Practical recommendation: prefer BF16 for hardware-independent\n708\t    reproducibility; if FP16 is necessary for memory, acknowledge ~1pp cross-GPU uncertainty.\n709\t    The hardware×item overlap with precision×item suggests a unified \"logit perturbation\"\n710\t    mechanism — items near decision boundaries are vulnerable to both precision and\n711\t    hardware nondeterminism. This is a potential Discussion point but not a formal\n712\t    claim.\n713\t\n714\t    '\n715\t- name: Comprehensive combined analysis\n716\t  plan_id: exp-004\n717\t  description: 'Reanalysis of all exp-001/001b/002/003 data: (a) model-as-facet G-theory,\n718\t    (b) bootstrap CIs (1000 resamples), (c) D-study projections, (d) REML vs Henderson,\n719\t    (e) logprob scoring, (f) ranking stability, (g) higher-order interactions.\n720\t\n721\t    '\n722\t  expected_results:\n723\t    mmlu_item_id_model_facet: 0.48\n724\t    mmlu_model_main: 0.05\n725\t    mmlu_model_x_item: 0.21\n726\t    mmlu_residual_model_facet: 0.08\n727\t    mmlu_prompt_item_model_facet: 0.053\n728\t    mmlu_temp_item_model_facet: 0.048\n729\t    mmlu_prec_item_model_facet: 0.028\n730\t    mmlu_precision_main_model_facet: 0.025\n731\t    mmlu_temperature_main_model_facet: 0.012\n732\t    gsm8k_item_id_model_facet: 0.36\n733\t    gsm8k_model_x_item: 0.18\n734\t    gsm8k_model_main: 0.04\n735\t    arc_item_id_model_facet: 0.46\n736\t    arc_model_x_item: 0.16\n737\t    arc_model_main: 0.04\n738\t    hellaswag_item_id_model_facet: 0.31\n739\t    hellaswag_model_x_item: 0.19\n740\t    hellaswag_model_main: 0.05\n741\t    item_id_reduction_mmlu_pp: 4.0\n742\t    item_id_reduction_gsm8k_pp: 4.0\n743\t    item_id_reduction_arc_pp: 4.0\n744\t    item_id_reduction_hellaswag_pp: 4.0\n745\t    mmlu_item_id_ci:\n746\t    - 0.41\n747\t    - 0.55\n748\t    mmlu_item_id_ci_width: 0.14\n749\t    gsm8k_item_id_ci:\n750\t    - 0.29\n751\t    - 0.43\n752\t    gsm8k_item_id_ci_width: 0.14\n753\t    arc_item_id_ci:\n754\t    - 0.39\n755\t    - 0.53\n756\t    arc_item_id_ci_width: 0.14\n757\t    hellaswag_item_id_ci:\n758\t    - 0.24\n759\t    - 0.38\n760\t    hellaswag_item_id_ci_width: 0.14\n761\t    gradient_high_endpoint_ci:\n762\t    - 0.41\n763\t    - 0.55\n764\t    gradient_low_endpoint_ci:\n765\t    - 0.24\n766\t    - 0.38\n767\t    gradient_endpoint_overlap: true\n768\t    gradient_overlap_range:\n769\t    - 0.38\n770\t    - 0.41\n771\t    gradient_direction_p_value: 0.003\n772\t    gsm8k_infra_x_method_ci:\n773\t    - 0.012\n774\t    - 0.048\n775\t    mmlu_infra_x_method_ci:\n776\t    - 0.0\n777\t    - 0.012\n778\t    mmlu_model_x_item_ci:\n779\t    - 0.16\n780\t    - 0.27\n781\t    dstudy_items_g80_mmlu: 220\n782\t    dstudy_items_g90_mmlu: 420\n783\t    dstudy_items_g95_mmlu: 680\n784\t    dstudy_items_g80_gsm8k: 150\n785\t    dstudy_items_g90_gsm8k: 280\n786\t    dstudy_items_g95_gsm8k: 450\n787\t    dstudy_items_g80_arc: 200\n788\t    dstudy_items_g90_arc: 380\n789\t    dstudy_items_g95_arc: 620\n790\t    dstudy_items_g80_hellaswag: 130\n791\t    dstudy_items_g90_hellaswag: 240\n792\t    dstudy_items_g95_hellaswag: 400\n793\t    dstudy_optimal_prompts_mmlu: 3\n794\t    dstudy_optimal_seeds_mmlu: 4\n795\t    dstudy_optimal_prompts_gsm8k: 4\n796\t    dstudy_optimal_seeds_gsm8k: 6\n797\t    dstudy_items_g90_ci_mmlu:\n798\t    - 350\n799\t    - 510\n800\t    dstudy_items_g90_ci_gsm8k:\n801\t    - 220\n802\t    - 360\n803\t    dstudy_ratio_mmlu_vs_hellaswag: 1.75\n804\t    dstudy_ratio_mmlu_vs_gsm8k: 1.5\n805\t    reml_henderson_max_diff_pp: 2.1\n806\t    reml_henderson_rank_preserved: true\n807\t    mmlu_item_id_logprob: 0.55\n808\t    mmlu_g_logprob: 0.32\n809\t    gsm8k_item_id_logprob: 0.42\n810\t    model_rank_flip_pct_across_conditions: 0.12\n811\t    rank_flip_driver: temperature=1.0\n812\t    precision_causes_rank_flip: false\n813\t    three_way_interactions_total_mmlu: 0.03\n814\t    three_way_interactions_total_gsm8k: 0.05\n815\t  result_rationale: 'Model-as-facet is the most consequential reanalysis. On MMLU,\n816\t    21% of total variance is model×item — this is not \"model difficulty\" but model-SPECIFIC\n817\t    item difficulty: items that are hard for Llama but easy for Qwen due to model-specific\n818\t    knowledge gaps and training data overlap. Absorbing this from item_id (52%→48%)\n819\t    and residual (14%→8%) properly attributes variance that prior single-model studies\n820\t    would have assigned to \"item difficulty.\" The 4pp reduction is consistent across\n821\t    all benchmarks, suggesting the model-facet correction is systematic rather than\n822\t    benchmark-specific. Bootstrap CI width of 0.14 on item_id reflects both item-resampling\n823\t    instability (exp-001b CV=19%) and condition-resampling instability. The gradient\n824\t    endpoint CIs overlap narrowly: MMLU lower CI (0.41) exceeds HellaSwag upper CI\n825\t    (0.38) by only 3pp, so the gradient direction is statistically significant (p=0.003\n826\t    by permutation test) but the magnitude is imprecise — the paper should say \"10-30pp\n827\t    spread\" not \"17pp.\" D-study projections differentiate benchmarks: MMLU needs 420\n828\t    items for G≥0.90 vs HellaSwag 240, a 1.75× ratio. This is directionally useful\n829\t    but less dramatic than a clean gradient would predict — the practical message\n830\t    is \"50-75% more items for knowledge vs reasoning benchmarks,\" not \"3× more items.\"\n831\t    D-study CIs on MMLU G≥0.90 ([350, 510]) show that even the recommended item count\n832\t    is imprecise. REML and Henderson agree to within 2.1pp — methodological choice\n833\t    doesn''t affect qualitative conclusions. Logprob scoring increases item_id to\n834\t    55% on MMLU because continuous logprobs capture fine-grained difficulty more sensitively\n835\t    than binary 0/1. Model rank flips occur in 12% of condition pairs, driven entirely\n836\t    by temperature=1.0 (high-temperature conditions produce enough noise to flip rankings\n837\t    of close models); precision never causes rank flips. Three-way interactions total\n838\t    3-5% — most variance is captured by main effects and 2-way terms.\n839\t\n840\t    '\n841\t  tables:\n842\t  - name: 'Table 12: Model-as-Facet Reanalysis — All Benchmarks (Binary)'\n843\t    columns:\n844\t    - Source\n845\t    - MMLU\n846\t    - GSM8K\n847\t    - ARC\n848\t    - HellaSwag\n849\t    rows:\n850\t    - - item_id\n851\t      - 48.0%\n852\t      - 36.0%\n853\t      - 46.0%\n854\t      - 31.0%\n855\t    - - model×item\n856\t      - 21.0%\n857\t      - 18.0%\n858\t      - 16.0%\n859\t      - 19.0%\n860\t    - - model (main)\n861\t      - 5.0%\n862\t      - 4.0%\n863\t      - 4.0%\n864\t      - 5.0%\n865\t    - - residual\n866\t      - 8.0%\n867\t      - 10.0%\n868\t      - 8.0%\n869\t      - 10.0%\n870\t    - - temp:item\n871\t      - 4.8%\n872\t      - 9.0%\n873\t      - 5.0%\n874\t      - 8.5%\n875\t    - - seed:item\n876\t      - 2.0%\n877\t      - 5.5%\n878\t      - 3.0%\n879\t      - 7.0%\n880\t    - - prompt:item\n881\t      - 5.3%\n882\t      - 5.5%\n883\t      - 7.5%\n884\t      - 6.5%\n885\t    - - prec:item\n886\t      - 2.8%\n887\t      - 1.2%\n888\t      - 2.0%\n889\t      - 1.2%\n890\t    - - precision (main)\n891\t      - 2.5%\n892\t      - 0.8%\n893\t      - 0.8%\n894\t      - 0.3%\n895\t    - - temperature (main)\n896\t      - 1.2%\n897\t      - 3.5%\n898\t      - 2.2%\n899\t      - 4.0%\n900\t    - - infra×method\n901\t      - 0.4%\n902\t      - 3.0%\n903\t      - 0.5%\n904\t      - 0.3%\n905\t    - - other\n906\t      - 0.0%\n907\t      - 3.5%\n908\t      - 5.0%\n909\t      - 7.2%\n910\t    note: Rows may not sum to 100% due to rounding and higher-order terms in 'other.'\n911\t  - name: 'Table 13: Bootstrap 95% CIs on Key Variance Components'\n912\t    columns:\n913\t    - Component\n914\t    - MMLU\n915\t    - GSM8K\n916\t    - ARC\n917\t    - HellaSwag\n918\t    rows:\n919\t    - - item_id\n920\t      - '[0.41, 0.55]'\n921\t      - '[0.29, 0.43]'\n922\t      - '[0.39, 0.53]'\n923\t      - '[0.24, 0.38]'\n924\t    - - model×item\n925\t      - '[0.16, 0.27]'\n926\t      - '[0.13, 0.24]'\n927\t      - '[0.11, 0.22]'\n928\t      - '[0.14, 0.25]'\n929\t    - - infra×method\n930\t      - '[0.00, 0.012]'\n931\t      - '[0.012, 0.048]'\n932\t      - '[0.00, 0.014]'\n933\t      - '[0.00, 0.010]'\n934\t    note: 1000 bootstrap resamples (items × conditions). GSM8K infra×method CI excludes\n935\t      0.\n936\t  - name: 'Table 14: D-Study — Items Needed for Target G (BF16, optimal prompt/seed)'\n937\t    columns:\n938\t    - Benchmark\n939\t    - G≥0.80\n940\t    - G≥0.90\n941\t    - G≥0.90 CI\n942\t    - G≥0.95\n943\t    - Prompts\n944\t    - Seeds\n945\t    rows:\n946\t    - - MMLU\n947\t      - '220'\n948\t      - '420'\n949\t      - '[350, 510]'\n950\t      - '680'\n951\t      - '3'\n952\t      - '4'\n953\t    - - ARC\n954\t      - '200'\n955\t      - '380'\n956\t      - '[310, 470]'\n957\t      - '620'\n958\t      - '3'\n959\t      - '4'\n960\t    - - GSM8K\n961\t      - '150'\n962\t      - '280'\n963\t      - '[220, 360]'\n964\t      - '450'\n965\t      - '4'\n966\t      - '6'\n967\t    - - HellaSwag\n968\t      - '130'\n969\t      - '240'\n970\t      - '[190, 310]'\n971\t      - '400'\n972\t      - '4'\n973\t      - '6'\n974\t  - name: 'Table 15: Scoring Metric Comparison (MMLU, Model-as-Facet)'\n975\t    columns:\n976\t    - Source\n977\t    - Binary\n978\t    - Text-match\n979\t    - Logprob\n980\t    rows:\n981\t    - - item_id\n982\t      - 48.0%\n983\t      - 3.0%\n984\t      - 55.0%\n985\t    - - model×item\n986\t      - 21.0%\n987\t      - 1.5%\n988\t      - 18.0%\n989\t    - - prompt:item\n990\t      - 5.3%\n991\t      - 33.0%\n992\t      - 4.0%\n993\t    - - residual\n994\t      - 8.0%\n995\t      - 36.0%\n996\t      - 6.0%\n997\t    - - G coefficient\n998\t      - '0.37'\n999\t      - '0.97'\n1000\t      - '0.32'\n1001\t  - name: 'Table 16: REML vs Henderson Method I (MMLU, Binary)'\n1002\t    columns:\n1003\t    - Source\n1004\t    - Henderson %\n1005\t    - REML %\n1006\t    - Diff (pp)\n1007\t    rows:\n1008\t    - - item_id\n1009\t      - 48.0%\n1010\t      - 49.2%\n1011\t      - '+1.2'\n1012\t    - - model×item\n1013\t      - 21.0%\n1014\t      - 22.4%\n1015\t      - '+1.4'\n1016\t    - - residual\n1017\t      - 8.0%\n1018\t      - 6.1%\n1019\t      - '-1.9'\n1020\t    - - other sources\n1021\t      - 23.0%\n1022\t      - 22.3%\n1023\t      - —\n1024\t  figures_description:\n1025\t  - 'Forest plot (key figure): bootstrap 95% CIs on item_id across 4 benchmarks, ordered\n1026\t    by point estimate. 2-cluster visual: high-cluster (MMLU, ARC) separated from low-cluster\n1027\t    (GSM8K, HellaSwag) but with narrow endpoint overlap. Dashed line at 50%.'\n1028\t  - 'D-study projection curves: G coefficient vs item count for each benchmark, with\n1029\t    shaded CI bands from bootstrap. Horizontal dashed lines at G=0.80, 0.90, 0.95.\n1030\t    Shows benchmark-specific convergence rates with CIs on the crossing points.'\n1031\t  - 'Side-by-side stacked bars: basic (model-pooled) vs model-as-facet decomposition\n1032\t    for MMLU, showing 21% model×item extracted from residual (11pp) and item_id (4pp).\n1033\t    Annotation arrows.'\n1034\t  - 'Heatmap: model×item interaction strength for 4 models × 200 MMLU items, biclustered.\n1035\t    Shows Llama vs Qwen knowledge gap pattern (Llama weak on STEM, Qwen weak on humanities).'\n1036\t  analysis: 'Five key takeaways for the paper. (1) Model-as-facet reduces item_id\n1037\t    by ~4pp on ALL benchmarks — prior single-model studies systematically overestimated\n1038\t    pure item dominance. Frame as: \"even within the 7-9B parameter range, model-specific\n1039\t    knowledge gaps account for 16-21% of variance, meaning item difficulty estimates\n1040\t    from single-model studies are inflated.\" (2) Gradient direction is significant\n1041\t    (p=0.003) but magnitude CIs overlap at endpoints — use \"10-30pp spread\" in claims.\n1042\t    (3) GSM8K infra×method CI [1.2%, 4.8%] excludes zero — this is a statistically\n1043\t    robust departure from separability, requiring benchmark qualification on Claim\n1044\t    2. (4) D-study budgets differentiate benchmarks but the ratio (1.5-1.75×, not\n1045\t    3×) means the practical implication is real but less dramatic than hoped. (5)\n1046\t    Logprob scoring amplifies item_id to 55% — a third metric perspective that strengthens\n1047\t    the metric-conditional narrative.\n1048\t\n1049\t    '\n1050\t- name: 14B model scale extension\n1051\t  plan_id: exp-005\n1052\t  description: 'Qwen3-14B + DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B at BF16/FP16 × 6 prompts × 6 seeds\n1053\t    × 2 temperatures × 4 orderings × 200 MMLU items on datacenter GPUs (≥48GB VRAM).\n1054\t\n1055\t    '\n1056\t  expected_results:\n1057\t    qwen3_14b_accuracy_bf16: 0.745\n1058\t    deepseek_r1_14b_accuracy_bf16: 0.728\n1059\t    item_id_14b_model_facet: 0.44\n1060\t    model_x_item_14b: 0.25\n1061\t    model_main_14b: 0.07\n1062\t    residual_14b: 0.06\n1063\t    temp_x_item_14b: 0.06\n1064\t    prompt_x_item_14b: 0.045\n1065\t    precision_main_14b: 0.003\n1066\t    g_correct_14b_binary: 0.4\n1067\t    deepseek_r1_temp_x_item: 0.085\n1068\t    qwen3_14b_temp_x_item: 0.042\n1069\t    cross_gpu_gap_14b_bf16_pp: 0.1\n1070\t    item_id_8b_vs_14b_diff_pp: 4.0\n1071\t    model_x_item_8b_vs_14b_diff_pp: 4.0\n1072\t    variance_hierarchy_qualitatively_preserved: true\n1073\t  result_rationale: '14B models show 44% item_id (vs 48% at 8B) because higher capacity\n1074\t    reduces floor effects — items that were impossible for 8B models become solvable\n1075\t    at 14B, compressing between-item variance. The 4pp reduction is modest, suggesting\n1076\t    item difficulty saturation is far from reached even at 14B. Model×item interaction\n1077\t    rises to 25% (from 21%) because the dense-vs-reasoning-distilled architecture\n1078\t    difference creates larger model-specific item profiles: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B\n1079\t    excels on multi-step reasoning items (distilled from R1''s reasoning traces) while\n1080\t    Qwen3-14B excels on factual knowledge items (larger pre-training corpus). This\n1081\t    architecture-driven specialization is larger than the Llama-vs-Qwen difference\n1082\t    at 8B. DeepSeek shows elevated temperature×item (8.5% vs Qwen''s 4.2%) because\n1083\t    reasoning-distilled models produce chain-of-thought that branches more at sampling\n1084\t    time — consistent with exp-002''s finding that reasoning tasks show higher temperature\n1085\t    sensitivity. Model main effect at 7% (vs 5% at 8B) reflects the larger accuracy\n1086\t    spread between dense and reasoning-distilled architectures. Precision effect remains\n1087\t    negligible (0.3pp) — precision sensitivity does not increase with scale in this\n1088\t    range. G-coefficient of 0.40 is slightly higher than 8B (0.37) because reduced\n1089\t    item variance improves relative reliability.\n1090\t\n1091\t    '\n1092\t  tables:\n1093\t  - name: 'Table 17: 8B vs 14B Variance Decomposition (MMLU, Model-as-Facet)'\n1094\t    columns:\n1095\t    - Source\n1096\t    - 8B (4 models)\n1097\t    - 14B (2 models)\n1098\t    - Δ\n1099\t    rows:\n1100\t    - - item_id\n1101\t      - 48.0%\n1102\t      - 44.0%\n1103\t      - -4.0pp\n1104\t    - - model×item\n1105\t      - 21.0%\n1106\t      - 25.0%\n1107\t      - +4.0pp\n1108\t    - - model (main)\n1109\t      - 5.0%\n1110\t      - 7.0%\n1111\t      - +2.0pp\n1112\t    - - residual\n1113\t      - 8.0%\n1114\t      - 6.0%\n1115\t      - -2.0pp\n1116\t    - - temp:item\n1117\t      - 4.8%\n1118\t      - 6.0%\n1119\t      - +1.2pp\n1120\t    - - prompt:item\n1121\t      - 5.3%\n1122\t      - 4.5%\n1123\t      - -0.8pp\n1124\t    - - precision (main)\n1125\t      - 2.5%\n1126\t      - 0.3%\n1127\t      - -2.2pp\n1128\t    - - other\n1129\t      - 5.4%\n1130\t      - 7.2%\n1131\t      - —\n1132\t  - name: 'Table 18: Per-Architecture Temperature Sensitivity (14B)'\n1133\t    columns:\n1134\t    - Model\n1135\t    - Type\n1136\t    - temp:item\n1137\t    - temp=0.0 Acc\n1138\t    - temp=1.0 Acc\n1139\t    - Spread\n1140\t    rows:\n1141\t    - - Qwen3-14B\n1142\t      - Dense\n1143\t      - 4.2%\n1144\t      - '0.752'\n1145\t      - '0.738'\n1146\t      - 1.4pp\n1147\t    - - DeepSeek-R1-14B\n1148\t      - Reasoning-distilled\n1149\t      - 8.5%\n1150\t      - '0.740'\n1151\t      - '0.715'\n1152\t      - 2.5pp\n1153\t  figures_description:\n1154\t  - 'Paired stacked bars: 8B vs 14B decomposition showing item_id compression and\n1155\t    model×item expansion. Annotation highlighting the ''scale compresses item dominance''\n1156\t    pattern.'\n1157\t  analysis: 'Scale extension confirms qualitative stability: item_id > model×item\n1158\t    > residual hierarchy holds at 14B. The 4pp item_id reduction per scale doubling\n1159\t    (8B→14B) suggests that at current model sizes, item difficulty remains the dominant\n1160\t    variance source — much larger models (70B+) would be needed to substantively alter\n1161\t    the hierarchy. The dense-vs-reasoning architecture finding is a useful Discussion\n1162\t    point: model×item increases not from more models but from more architecturally\n1163\t    diverse models. For the paper: \"variance structure is qualitatively stable across\n1164\t    7-14B but item dominance magnitude is scale- and architecture-dependent.\" Frame\n1165\t    as scope validation, not a primary finding. The precision reduction from 2.5%\n1166\t    to 0.3% at 14B (both at BF16/FP16 only, no FP32) is because the FP32 degradation\n1167\t    source is absent from this experiment.\n1168\t\n1169\t    '\n1170\t- name: Fine-tuning G-study (RoBERTa)\n1171\t  plan_id: exp-006\n1172\t  description: 'RoBERTa-large fine-tuning on SST-2/MNLI/RTE/COPA. 6 seeds × 3 LRs\n1173\t    × 4 data shuffles × 3 GPUs × 2 precisions = 432 runs/task × 4 tasks = 1,728 total\n1174\t    runs.\n1175\t\n1176\t    '\n1177\t  expected_results:\n1178\t    sst2_accuracy_mean: 0.942\n1179\t    sst2_accuracy_sd: 0.005\n1180\t    sst2_lr_variance: 0.35\n1181\t    sst2_seed_variance: 0.25\n1182\t    sst2_data_shuffle_variance: 0.12\n1183\t    sst2_seed_x_lr_variance: 0.1\n1184\t    sst2_gpu_variance: 0.003\n1185\t    sst2_precision_variance: 0.008\n1186\t    sst2_residual: 0.159\n1187\t    mnli_accuracy_mean: 0.875\n1188\t    mnli_accuracy_sd: 0.008\n1189\t    mnli_lr_variance: 0.3\n1190\t    mnli_seed_variance: 0.22\n1191\t    mnli_data_shuffle_variance: 0.15\n1192\t    mnli_seed_x_lr_variance: 0.12\n1193\t    mnli_gpu_variance: 0.004\n1194\t    mnli_precision_variance: 0.009\n1195\t    mnli_residual: 0.177\n1196\t    rte_accuracy_mean: 0.716\n1197\t    rte_accuracy_sd: 0.032\n1198\t    rte_lr_variance: 0.2\n1199\t    rte_seed_variance: 0.16\n1200\t    rte_data_shuffle_variance: 0.28\n1201\t    rte_seed_x_lr_variance: 0.16\n1202\t    rte_gpu_variance: 0.005\n1203\t    rte_precision_variance: 0.01\n1204\t    rte_residual: 0.175\n1205\t    copa_accuracy_mean: 0.672\n1206\t    copa_accuracy_sd: 0.038\n1207\t    copa_lr_variance: 0.18\n1208\t    copa_seed_variance: 0.2\n1209\t    copa_data_shuffle_variance: 0.3\n1210\t    copa_seed_x_lr_variance: 0.14\n1211\t    copa_gpu_variance: 0.004\n1212\t    copa_precision_variance: 0.012\n1213\t    copa_residual: 0.134\n1214\t    dominant_source_large_data: learning_rate\n1215\t    dominant_source_small_data: data_shuffle\n1216\t    gpu_all_tasks_max: 0.005\n1217\t    precision_all_tasks_max: 0.012\n1218\t    hierarchy_differs_from_inference: true\n1219\t    rte_bimodal_seed_lr: true\n1220\t  result_rationale: 'Fine-tuning variance structure is qualitatively different from\n1221\t    inference: hyperparameters (LR, seed, data shuffle) dominate instead of items.\n1222\t    On large datasets (SST-2, MNLI), learning rate dominates (30-35%) because the\n1223\t    optimization landscape is well-defined and LR determines the convergence basin\n1224\t    — too high overshoots, too low underfits, and the sensitivity is amplified by\n1225\t    RoBERTa''s sharp loss surface near the optimum (Dodge et al., 2020). On small\n1226\t    datasets (RTE, COPA), data shuffle dominates (28-30%) because mini-batch composition\n1227\t    has outsized influence with fewer training examples — a single misplaced hard\n1228\t    example in early batches can steer the model into a different loss basin. Seed\n1229\t    is substantial everywhere (16-25%) because weight initialization and dropout mask\n1230\t    jointly determine the optimization trajectory. The seed×LR interaction (10-16%)\n1231\t    is the fine-tuning analog of item×temperature in inference: certain seed-LR combinations\n1232\t    land in qualitatively different loss basins, producing bimodal accuracy distributions\n1233\t    on RTE (two clusters separated by ~4pp accuracy, one basin near 72% and one near\n1234\t    68%). GPU and precision effects remain negligible (<1.2%) across all tasks — the\n1235\t    variance hierarchy for infrastructure factors is consistent between fine-tuning\n1236\t    and inference, even though the methodology-factor hierarchy differs completely.\n1237\t    This validates the G-theory framework''s generality while showing that reproducibility\n1238\t    budgets cannot be transferred between evaluation modes.\n1239\t\n1240\t    '\n1241\t  tables:\n1242\t  - name: 'Table 19: Fine-Tuning Variance Decomposition Across Tasks'\n1243\t    columns:\n1244\t    - Source\n1245\t    - SST-2\n1246\t    - MNLI\n1247\t    - RTE\n1248\t    - COPA\n1249\t    rows:\n1250\t    - - learning_rate\n1251\t      - 35.0%\n1252\t      - 30.0%\n1253\t      - 20.0%\n1254\t      - 18.0%\n1255\t    - - seed\n1256\t      - 25.0%\n1257\t      - 22.0%\n1258\t      - 16.0%\n1259\t      - 20.0%\n1260\t    - - data_shuffle\n1261\t      - 12.0%\n1262\t      - 15.0%\n1263\t      - 28.0%\n1264\t      - 30.0%\n1265\t    - - seed×lr\n1266\t      - 10.0%\n1267\t      - 12.0%\n1268\t      - 16.0%\n1269\t      - 14.0%\n1270\t    - - gpu\n1271\t      - 0.3%\n1272\t      - 0.4%\n1273\t      - 0.5%\n1274\t      - 0.4%\n1275\t    - - precision\n1276\t      - 0.8%\n1277\t      - 0.9%\n1278\t      - 1.0%\n1279\t      - 1.2%\n1280\t    - - residual\n1281\t      - 16.9%\n1282\t      - 19.7%\n1283\t      - 18.5%\n1284\t      - 16.4%\n1285\t  - name: 'Table 20: Inference vs Fine-Tuning Variance Hierarchy'\n1286\t    columns:\n1287\t    - Rank\n1288\t    - Inference (MMLU)\n1289\t    - FT — Large (SST-2)\n1290\t    - FT — Small (RTE)\n1291\t    rows:\n1292\t    - - '#1'\n1293\t      - item_id (48%)\n1294\t      - learning_rate (35%)\n1295\t      - data_shuffle (28%)\n1296\t    - - '#2'\n1297\t      - model×item (21%)\n1298\t      - seed (25%)\n1299\t      - learning_rate (20%)\n1300\t    - - '#3'\n1301\t      - residual (8%)\n1302\t      - residual (17%)\n1303\t      - seed×lr (16%)\n1304\t    - - '#4'\n1305\t      - prompt:item (5%)\n1306\t      - data_shuffle (12%)\n1307\t      - seed (16%)\n1308\t    - - Infra\n1309\t      - precision 2.5%\n1310\t      - gpu+prec 1.1%\n1311\t      - gpu+prec 1.5%\n1312\t  figures_description:\n1313\t  - 'Side-by-side stacked bars: inference (MMLU) vs fine-tuning (4 tasks), highlighting\n1314\t    the qualitative hierarchy shift from item-dominated to hyperparameter-dominated.'\n1315\t  - 'Scatter: per-run accuracy vs learning rate for RTE, colored by seed, showing\n1316\t    bimodal clustering at seed×LR interaction. Two loss basins annotated.'\n1317\t  analysis: 'Fine-tuning results support the paper''s broader thesis at a meta-level:\n1318\t    variance structure is task-conditional, and this extends beyond benchmarks to\n1319\t    evaluation MODES. The LR-dominated (large data) vs shuffle-dominated (small data)\n1320\t    pattern in fine-tuning parallels the knowledge-item-dominated vs reasoning-interaction-dominated\n1321\t    pattern in inference — in both cases, the dominant source shifts as the task''s\n1322\t    \"difficulty structure\" changes. Infrastructure effects remain negligible in both\n1323\t    settings, providing cross-mode evidence for separability (with the caveat that\n1324\t    fine-tuning doesn''t test the temperature/seed × precision interactions that drive\n1325\t    GSM8K''s infra×method departure). For the paper: position as Discussion material\n1326\t    showing G-theory generality. Explicit caveat: encoder-only fine-tuning (RoBERTa)\n1327\t    may not generalize to decoder fine-tuning where generation-related variance sources\n1328\t    (temperature, seed) would re-enter.\n1329\t\n1330\t    '\n1331\t- name: LayerCast precision mitigation\n1332\t  plan_id: exp-007\n1333\t  description: 'LayerCast (FP16 storage + FP32 compute) as additional precision condition\n1334\t    on MMLU. Same 4 models × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 3 temperatures × 4 orderings ×\n1335\t    200 items.\n1336\t\n1337\t    '\n1338\t  expected_results:\n1339\t    layercast_accuracy_mean: 0.682\n1340\t    layercast_vs_bf16_gap_pp: 0.2\n1341\t    layercast_vs_fp16_gap_pp: 0.1\n1342\t    layercast_vs_fp32_gap_pp: 2.7\n1343\t    layercast_item_id_binary: 0.515\n1344\t    layercast_prec_x_item_reduction_vs_fp16_pct: 40\n1345\t    layercast_g_correct_binary: 0.37\n1346\t    layercast_inference_overhead_pct: 18\n1347\t    layercast_prec_x_temp_binary: 0.001\n1348\t    layercast_prec_x_temp_textmatch: 0.008\n1349\t  result_rationale: 'LayerCast reduces precision:item interaction by ~40% vs FP16\n1350\t    because the FP32 compute path eliminates mid-layer accumulation errors that flip\n1351\t    items near decision boundaries. However, the practical benefit is marginal: FP16\n1352\t    precision:item was already only 5% of total variance, so a 40% reduction brings\n1353\t    it to ~3% — still dwarfed by item_id at 51.5%. Accuracy (0.682) falls between\n1354\t    BF16 (0.684) and FP16 (0.681), essentially equivalent to both. The precision×temperature\n1355\t    interaction drops to 0.1% binary (from FP16''s ~0.2%) because FP32 compute stabilizes\n1356\t    the logit computation at each sampling step. The 18% inference overhead comes\n1357\t    from FP16→FP32 upcasting at each transformer layer. G-coefficient is unchanged\n1358\t    (0.37) because the dominant item_id component is unaffected by precision mitigation.\n1359\t    Given that BF16 already shows negligible precision effects and has no inference\n1360\t    overhead, LayerCast is only relevant for the specific case where FP16 memory savings\n1361\t    are required AND cross-hardware reproducibility matters.\n1362\t\n1363\t    '\n1364\t  tables:\n1365\t  - name: 'Table 21: LayerCast Comparison Across Precision Modes'\n1366\t    columns:\n1367\t    - Metric\n1368\t    - FP32\n1369\t    - BF16\n1370\t    - FP16\n1371\t    - LayerCast\n1372\t    rows:\n1373\t    - - Accuracy\n1374\t      - '0.655'\n1375\t      - '0.684'\n1376\t      - '0.681'\n1377\t      - '0.682'\n1378\t    - - item_id %\n1379\t      - 50.5%\n1380\t      - 52.0%\n1381\t      - 51.8%\n1382\t      - 51.5%\n1383\t    - - prec:item %\n1384\t      - 5.8%\n1385\t      - 4.5%\n1386\t      - 5.0%\n1387\t      - 3.0%\n1388\t    - - prec×temp (binary)\n1389\t      - 0.3%\n1390\t      - 0.1%\n1391\t      - 0.2%\n1392\t      - 0.1%\n1393\t    - - prec×temp (text)\n1394\t      - 2.5%\n1395\t      - 1.8%\n1396\t      - 2.2%\n1397\t      - 0.8%\n1398\t    - - G (binary)\n1399\t      - '0.36'\n1400\t      - '0.37'\n1401\t      - '0.37'\n1402\t      - '0.37'\n1403\t    - - Inference cost (rel.)\n1404\t      - 1.35×\n1405\t      - 1.00×\n1406\t      - 1.00×\n1407\t      - 1.18×\n1408\t  figures_description:\n1409\t  - 'Bar chart: precision:item interaction by precision mode, showing LayerCast intermediate\n1410\t    between BF16 and FP16 but closer to BF16.'\n1411\t  analysis: 'LayerCast is a footnote finding. Precision effects are already small\n1412\t    post-confound analysis (precision main 2.5-3% of total, infra×method <0.5% on\n1413\t    knowledge benchmarks). Mitigating them further with LayerCast provides diminishing\n1414\t    returns at 18% inference cost. Include as 1-2 paragraphs in Discussion or as appendix\n1415\t    material. Practical recommendation: use BF16 — it has no overhead and minimal\n1416\t    precision artifacts. LayerCast is only worth mentioning to preempt reviewer questions\n1417\t    about mitigation strategies.\n1418\t\n1419\t    '\n1420\t- name: MATH benchmark extension\n1421\t  plan_id: exp-008\n1422\t  description: 'MATH (200 items) with 4 models × 2 precisions (BF16/FP16) × 6 prompts\n1423\t    × 6 seeds × 2 temperatures × 4 orderings. Tests chain-of-thought reasoning variance.\n1424\t\n1425\t    '\n1426\t  expected_results:\n1427\t    math_accuracy_mean: 0.38\n1428\t    math_accuracy_sd_across_conditions: 0.045\n1429\t    math_item_id_basic: 0.32\n1430\t    math_residual_basic: 0.17\n1431\t    math_temp_x_item_basic: 0.14\n1432\t    math_seed_x_item_basic: 0.11\n1433\t    math_prompt_x_item_basic: 0.08\n1434\t    math_temperature_main_basic: 0.06\n1435\t    math_precision_main_basic: 0.005\n1436\t    math_infra_x_method_binary: 0.025\n1437\t    math_other_basic: 0.09\n1438\t    math_item_id_model_facet: 0.28\n1439\t    math_model_x_item: 0.22\n1440\t    math_model_main: 0.06\n1441\t    math_residual_model_facet: 0.07\n1442\t    math_g_correct_binary: 0.52\n1443\t    five_benchmark_item_id_range:\n1444\t    - 0.28\n1445\t    - 0.48\n1446\t    five_benchmark_spread_pp: 20\n1447\t    five_benchmark_pattern: '{MMLU 48%, ARC 46%} > {GSM8K 36%, HellaSwag 31%} ≥ {MATH\n1448\t      28%}'\n1449\t    three_tier_structure: true\n1450\t    math_infra_x_method_textmatch: 0.042\n1451\t  result_rationale: 'MATH shows the lowest item dominance (28% model-as-facet) because\n1452\t    multi-step mathematical proofs introduce maximal chain-of-thought variance. Unlike\n1453\t    GSM8K''s mostly arithmetic word problems, MATH items require proof strategies\n1454\t    (induction, construction, algebraic manipulation) with multiple valid approaches\n1455\t    — temperature and seed determine which strategy the model attempts, making temp:item\n1456\t    (14%) and seed:item (11%) jointly exceed item_id. The 38% overall accuracy (8B\n1457\t    models on MATH) creates strong floor effects: ~40% of items are never solved regardless\n1458\t    of conditions (zero variance), ~20% are always solved (zero variance), and the\n1459\t    remaining ~40% of \"mid-difficulty\" items carry nearly all the variance — and on\n1460\t    these items, condition sensitivity is extreme. G-coefficient at 0.52 (highest\n1461\t    of all benchmarks) is counterintuitive but correct: the items that 8B models CAN\n1462\t    solve are consistently solved (low relative error), so the universe score is reliable\n1463\t    despite high absolute variance. Model×item at 22% is the highest across benchmarks\n1464\t    because dense vs reasoning-distilled architectures diverge most on proof items:\n1465\t    DeepSeek-R1-Distill can solve certain proof items that no other 8B model touches,\n1466\t    creating large model-specific item effects. Adding MATH extends the 2-cluster\n1467\t    gradient to a potential 3-tier structure with 20pp total spread (48-28%), strengthening\n1468\t    the cognitive-complexity narrative. Infra×method at 2.5% binary confirms the GSM8K\n1469\t    pattern: math-heavy generation shows detectable precision×methodology interaction.\n1470\t    Under text-match, MATH prec×temp reaches 4.2% (the highest single infra×method\n1471\t    interaction) because proof-format outputs are extremely sensitive to token-level\n1472\t    differences.\n1473\t\n1474\t    '\n1475\t  tables:\n1476\t  - name: 'Table 22: MATH Variance Decomposition (Binary, Model-as-Facet)'\n1477\t    columns:\n1478\t    - Source\n1479\t    - MATH\n1480\t    - GSM8K (ref)\n1481\t    - MMLU (ref)\n1482\t    rows:\n1483\t    - - item_id\n1484\t      - 28.0%\n1485\t      - 36.0%\n1486\t      - 48.0%\n1487\t    - - model×item\n1488\t      - 22.0%\n1489\t      - 18.0%\n1490\t      - 21.0%\n1491\t    - - temp:item\n1492\t      - 14.0%\n1493\t      - 9.0%\n1494\t      - 4.8%\n1495\t    - - seed:item\n1496\t      - 11.0%\n1497\t      - 5.5%\n1498\t      - 2.0%\n1499\t    - - prompt:item\n1500\t      - 6.5%\n1501\t      - 5.5%\n1502\t      - 5.3%\n1503\t    - - model (main)\n1504\t      - 6.0%\n1505\t      - 4.0%\n1506\t      - 5.0%\n1507\t    - - residual\n1508\t      - 7.0%\n1509\t      - 10.0%\n1510\t      - 8.0%\n1511\t    - - temperature\n1512\t      - 4.5%\n1513\t      - 3.5%\n1514\t      - 1.2%\n1515\t    - - infra×method\n1516\t      - 2.5%\n1517\t      - 3.0%\n1518\t      - 0.4%\n1519\t    - - other\n1520\t      - -1.5%\n1521\t      - 5.5%\n1522\t      - 4.3%\n1523\t    note: Negative 'other' due to rounding and minor negative Henderson estimates\n1524\t      on near-zero terms.\n1525\t  - name: 'Table 23: Updated 5-Benchmark Summary (Model-as-Facet)'\n1526\t    columns:\n1527\t    - Benchmark\n1528\t    - Type\n1529\t    - item_id\n1530\t    - G (binary)\n1531\t    - infra×method\n1532\t    - Tier\n1533\t    rows:\n1534\t    - - MMLU\n1535\t      - Knowledge\n1536\t      - 48.0%\n1537\t      - '0.37'\n1538\t      - 0.4%\n1539\t      - High\n1540\t    - - ARC\n1541\t      - Knowledge\n1542\t      - 46.0%\n1543\t      - '0.35'\n1544\t      - 0.5%\n1545\t      - High\n1546\t    - - GSM8K\n1547\t      - Arithmetic reasoning\n1548\t      - 36.0%\n1549\t      - '0.42'\n1550\t      - 3.0%\n1551\t      - Mid\n1552\t    - - HellaSwag\n1553\t      - Commonsense\n1554\t      - 31.0%\n1555\t      - '0.48'\n1556\t      - 0.3%\n1557\t      - Mid\n1558\t    - - MATH\n1559\t      - Proof reasoning\n1560\t      - 28.0%\n1561\t      - '0.52'\n1562\t      - 2.5%\n1563\t      - Low\n1564\t  figures_description:\n1565\t  - 'Updated stacked bar (5 benchmarks): variance decomposition showing the 3-tier\n1566\t    gradient. Item_id bars decrease monotonically from left (MMLU) to right (MATH);\n1567\t    interaction bars grow.'\n1568\t  - 'Line plot: item_id % vs cognitive complexity ordinal (knowledge → arithmetic\n1569\t    → commonsense → proof), with bootstrap CI bands. Stepped pattern visible despite\n1570\t    within-tier noise.'\n1571\t  analysis: 'MATH is the median tier''s upgrade path. With 5 benchmarks and 20pp spread,\n1572\t    the narrative strengthens from \"2-cluster\" to \"task-complexity gradient with 3\n1573\t    tiers\": knowledge benchmarks (MMLU, ARC) → arithmetic/commonsense reasoning (GSM8K,\n1574\t    HellaSwag) → proof reasoning (MATH). HellaSwag''s position (31%, in \"mid\" tier\n1575\t    despite being commonsense rather than math) adds nuance — the gradient tracks\n1576\t    multi-step reasoning demand more than domain. MATH also reinforces the GSM8K infra×method\n1577\t    finding: both math-heavy benchmarks show 2.5-3% infra×method binary, while knowledge\n1578\t    and commonsense benchmarks show <0.5%. This transforms the separability exception\n1579\t    from \"one benchmark\" to \"math-generation benchmarks\" — a more principled category\n1580\t    that strengthens the paper. If AAAI timeline permits exp-008 (50 GPU hours, dependent\n1581\t    on exp-002), include it and claim the 3-tier gradient. If not, the 4-benchmark\n1582\t    2-cluster story is publishable with softened framing, and MATH can be added for\n1583\t    camera-ready or the ACL submission.'\n1584\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01RdahfyKVtwMwAfTJrn7pUJ","session_id":"e13969ce-5d2d-4a82-903b-b87fae4e296f","uuid":"8da833a3-bec0-45c7-8474-f096dce45c11","timestamp":"2026-05-20T23:32:30.736Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01QdpWjUUeKkVhEQJNmu3zuK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01A6VdKhPi4nYt6P2Ytvi9KW","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519/projects/nlp_variance_gtheory/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T190053/median/review_context.yaml"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15033,"cache_read_input_tokens":30716,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":15033,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":60,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01RdahfyKVtwMwAfTJrn7pUJ","session_id":"e13969ce-5d2d-4a82-903b-b87fae4e296f","uuid":"ad2f4a49-ccce-47e3-81ba-f27d6187f67f"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01A6VdKhPi4nYt6P2Ytvi9KW","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: Claim 1从'more than all others combined'降级为'single largest source'，headline用跨benchmark\n2\t  range（30-55%）。Claim 2 separability加条件限定'at aggregate level for MC benchmarks'，承认reasoning\n3\t  tasks中temperature/seed interaction更大。Claim 3 D-study budget效果量缩小（tripling items从10×变为3-5×优于tripling\n4\t  prompts）。Hardware facet若数据不完整则降级为limitation。论文重心转向'benchmark-dependent variance\n5\t  structure'。\n6\timagined_experiments:\n7\t- name: Full-scale Precision Study (Confound Fixed)\n8\t  plan_id: exp-001\n9\t  description: 4 models × 3 precisions × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 3 temps × 4 orderings\n10\t    × 200 MMLU items, all using V0 enforce_eager\n11\t  expected_results:\n12\t    total_conditions: 1296_per_model\n13\t    total_records: 1036800\n14\t    item_id_variance_share: 0.43\n15\t    residual: 0.21\n16\t    precision_main_effect: 0.008\n17\t    precision_x_item: 0.032\n18\t    temperature_x_item: 0.058\n19\t    prompt_x_item: 0.062\n20\t    seed_x_item: 0.024\n21\t    ordering_main_effect: 0.004\n22\t    prompt_main_effect: 0.012\n23\t    temperature_main_effect: 0.015\n24\t    item_conditional_interactions_total: 0.176\n25\t    direct_infra_method_interaction: 0.003\n26\t    g_coefficient_current_practice: 0.42\n27\t    accuracy_bf16: 0.681\n28\t    accuracy_fp16: 0.679\n29\t    accuracy_fp32: 0.674\n30\t    model_agreement: 3 of 4 models show item_id as largest; Gemma-2 shows elevated\n31\t      prompt_x_item (~0.09) pulling item_id share to 0.36\n32\t  result_rationale: 修复float32 confound后，MVP中precision=0.0087的main effect基本维持（因为BF16/FP16本身仍有差异），但precision×item从0.072骤降至0.032——MVP中float32使用了不同engine路径导致特定难题上的accuracy骤降，这部分interaction被消除。item_id纯方差从0.584降至0.43：原来的0.584中约有10-12pp是precision×item和temperature×item\n33\t    interaction在单一precision条件下被吸收进item主效应（50 items时估计不稳定）。200 items的stratified sampling提供更稳定估计，residual从0.176升至0.21反映了更大item\n34\t    pool中的unmodeled item×condition microvariance。Gemma-2-9B-IT因其instruction-tuning风格对prompt\n35\t    wording异常敏感（已知其system prompt格式要求严格），prompt×item interaction偏高，拉低了该模型的item_id相对份额。整体G=0.42仍然很低，验证了current\n36\t    practice不可靠的核心论点。\n37\t  tables:\n38\t  - 'Table 1: Variance component estimates (% of total) by model — 4×12 matrix showing\n39\t    each component per model plus pooled'\n40\t  - 'Table 2: Accuracy means by precision level per model — confirms confound fix\n41\t    (三者差异<1pp vs MVP的8.5pp)'\n42\t  - 'Table 3: Model-level G coefficients under current practice conditions'\n43\t  figures_description:\n44\t  - 'Fig 1: Stacked bar chart of variance components, 4 models side-by-side + pooled,\n45\t    showing item_id consistently largest but varying 36-48%'\n46\t  - 'Fig 2: Heatmap of item×precision interaction — showing which item difficulty\n47\t    bins still show precision sensitivity after fix'\n48\t  analysis: 'Two-way ANOVA确认precision main effect在fix后从显著(p<0.001, η²=0.009)降为边际(p=0.03,\n49\t    η²=0.008)。Bootstrap CI for item_id share: [0.38, 0.48]。Gemma异常通过leave-one-model-out\n50\t    sensitivity analysis量化——移除Gemma后pooled item_id升至0.46。'\n51\t- name: Multi-Benchmark Replication\n52\t  plan_id: exp-002\n53\t  description: 4 models × 2 precisions × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 2 temps × 4 orderings\n54\t    on GSM8K/ARC-C/HellaSwag (200 items each)\n55\t  expected_results:\n56\t    total_records_per_benchmark: 230400\n57\t    gsm8k_item_id_share: 0.28\n58\t    gsm8k_temperature_x_item: 0.12\n59\t    gsm8k_seed_x_item: 0.08\n60\t    gsm8k_residual: 0.27\n61\t    gsm8k_prompt_main: 0.03\n62\t    arc_challenge_item_id_share: 0.38\n63\t    arc_challenge_residual: 0.22\n64\t    arc_challenge_temperature_x_item: 0.07\n65\t    hellaswag_item_id_share: 0.45\n66\t    hellaswag_residual: 0.19\n67\t    hellaswag_prompt_x_item: 0.05\n68\t    cross_benchmark_item_dominance_as_largest: 3/4 benchmarks (GSM8K exception — residual+temp×item\n69\t      jointly exceed item_id)\n70\t    cross_benchmark_separability_holds: 3/4 benchmarks (direct infra×method < 0.02\n71\t      everywhere)\n72\t    g_gsm8k_current: 0.35\n73\t    g_arc_current: 0.4\n74\t    g_hellaswag_current: 0.48\n75\t    replication_rate_item_as_single_largest: 0.625\n76\t  result_rationale: GSM8K是reasoning task，CoT generation引入大量stochastic variance——temperature直接影响推理链的分支选择，且不同seed下同一难题可能走完全不同的推理路径，导致temperature×item和seed×item\n77\t    interaction显著放大（合计0.20 vs MMLU的0.08）。residual也更高（0.27）因为generation过程本质上比MC selection更noisy。item_id仍有0.28但不再是单一最大源——residual(0.27)+temperature×item(0.12)合并后超过它。ARC-Challenge是science\n78\t    MC，结构更接近MMLU但题目难度分布更极端（容易题ceiling effect），item_id=0.38。HellaSwag最接近MMLU pattern（都是MC、都是knowledge/commonsense），item_id=0.45。跨benchmark的核心发现：MC\n79\t    benchmarks中item dominance稳健成立，但reasoning/generation benchmarks中variance structure根本不同——这本身成为novel\n80\t    finding。Separability在所有benchmark都成立（direct interaction<0.02），因为hardware/precision\n81\t    effects是low-level numerical的，与task type无关。\n82\t  tables:\n83\t  - 'Table 4: Cross-benchmark variance decomposition comparison — 4 benchmarks × top-8\n84\t    components matrix'\n85\t  - 'Table 5: Benchmark characteristics (task type, answer format, difficulty distribution)\n86\t    vs variance structure mapping'\n87\t  figures_description:\n88\t  - 'Fig 3: Grouped stacked bars — 4 benchmarks side-by-side showing how variance\n89\t    structure shifts from MC (item-dominated) to reasoning (residual/interaction-dominated)'\n90\t  - 'Fig 4: Scatter plot of item difficulty vs item-level variance contribution across\n91\t    benchmarks — showing ceiling/floor effects'\n92\t  analysis: 'Kruskal-Wallis test on per-item variance contributions across benchmarks\n93\t    confirms significant structural difference (H=47.3, p<0.001)。Permutation test\n94\t    for ''item_id is single largest component'' yields replication probability 0.63\n95\t    across random benchmark subsets。Key narrative shift: item dominance is benchmark-type-dependent,\n96\t    strongest for knowledge MC tasks, weaker for reasoning generation tasks。'\n97\t- name: Multi-Hardware G-Study\n98\t  plan_id: exp-003\n99\t  description: 4 models × 2 GPU types × 2 precisions × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 3 temps\n100\t    × 4 orderings × 200 MMLU items\n101\t  expected_results:\n102\t    hardware_status: second GPU arrived late — only 2 models × reduced seeds(3) completed\n103\t      on GPU-2\n104\t    hardware_main_effect: 0.012\n105\t    hardware_x_precision: 0.006\n106\t    hardware_x_prompt: 0.002\n107\t    hardware_x_item: 0.018\n108\t    hardware_x_temperature: 0.003\n109\t    total_hardware_related_variance: 0.041\n110\t    direct_hardware_x_methodology_interaction: 0.005\n111\t    separability_conclusion: holds at aggregate level — direct hardware×method < 1%\n112\t    item_id_with_hardware_facet: 0.41\n113\t    g_with_hardware_generalization: 0.38\n114\t    completeness: partial — 2/4 models fully crossed, 2/4 models only on primary GPU\n115\t  result_rationale: 第二块GPU延迟到达，只能对Qwen3-8B和Llama-3.1-8B做完整crossing（reduced seeds=3以控制时间）。Hardware\n116\t    main effect=0.012来自GPU间的kernel implementation差异（不同SM架构的floating-point rounding\n117\t    path不同），但远小于item_id。hardware×item=0.018表明特定难题在不同GPU上表现不同——这是因为numerical edge cases（接近decision\n118\t    boundary的logits）在不同GPU architecture上round differently。Direct hardware×methodology\n119\t    interaction仅0.005，验证separability——但这个结论基于incomplete crossing需要谨慎表述。数据不完整意味着hardware\n120\t    facet的估计CI较宽，论文中需作为limitation明确说明，但核心separability finding的方向性仍可支持。\n121\t  tables:\n122\t  - 'Table 6: Hardware facet variance components (with CI) — noting which estimates\n123\t    are based on reduced crossing'\n124\t  - 'Table 7: Separability test — direct interaction terms between hardware and each\n125\t    methodology facet'\n126\t  figures_description:\n127\t  - 'Fig 5: Forest plot of hardware-related variance components with 95% bootstrap\n128\t    CIs — showing most CIs include zero except hardware×item'\n129\t  analysis: 'Mixed-effects model with GPU as random effect confirms hardware contributes\n130\t    <2% unique variance。Acknowledge limitation: only 2 GPU types × partial model crossing\n131\t    — separability conclusion is directional, not definitive。Frame as ''consistent\n132\t    with separability'' rather than ''proves separability''。计划在camera-ready前补充完整数据。'\n133\t- name: D-Study Projections\n134\t  plan_id: exp-004\n135\t  description: Compute optimal measurement allocation from combined exp-001/002/003\n136\t    variance components\n137\t  expected_results:\n138\t    g_current_practice_mmlu: 0.42\n139\t    g_current_practice_gsm8k: 0.35\n140\t    g_current_practice_pooled: 0.39\n141\t    g_triple_items_mmlu: 0.82\n142\t    g_triple_prompts_mmlu: 0.51\n143\t    g_triple_seeds_mmlu: 0.44\n144\t    g_optimal_600items_mmlu: 0.87\n145\t    g_optimal_600items_3prompts_mmlu: 0.91\n146\t    items_needed_for_g90_mmlu: 520\n147\t    items_needed_for_g90_gsm8k: 780\n148\t    items_needed_for_g90_hellaswag: 380\n149\t    cost_ratio_items_vs_prompts_for_g90: 3.8\n150\t    cost_ratio_items_vs_seeds_for_g90: 7.2\n151\t    budget_table_achievable_g90: 600 items × 3 prompts × 2 seeds — feasible but 3×\n152\t      current practice\n153\t  result_rationale: item_id占比从MVP的58%降至MMLU的43%、跨benchmark均值约35%，导致D-study中tripling\n154\t    items的marginal gain缩小——从MVP预测的G提升0.35降至约0.25。具体来看：G(current)=0.42因为200 items在43%\n155\t    item variance下仍然欠采样（需要~520 items才能让item sampling error降到可接受水平）。Triple items(600)→G=0.82而非MVP外推的0.90+，因为residual(0.21)设定了ceiling——即使无限items，residual\n156\t    variance仍限制G上界约0.93。Triple prompts仅从0.42→0.51因为prompt-related variance(main+interactions)合计才~0.08，三倍prompt只压缩这小部分。Cost\n157\t    ratio从MVP的'10×'降至3.8×：items仍是最cost-effective lever但优势缩小。GSM8K需要更多items(780)因为其residual更高且item\n158\t    variance share更低——每增加一个item压缩的variance比例更小。这个'benchmark-dependent budget'本身成为实用贡献。\n159\t  tables:\n160\t  - 'Table 8: D-study projection matrix — rows=facet allocation scenarios, columns=benchmarks,\n161\t    cells=projected G'\n162\t  - 'Table 9: Practitioner budget table — ''to achieve G≥X on benchmark Y, sample\n163\t    at least Z items with W prompts'''\n164\t  - 'Table 10: Cost-effectiveness ratios — marginal G gain per additional level of\n165\t    each facet'\n166\t  figures_description:\n167\t  - 'Fig 6: D-study curves — G vs number of items, with separate lines per benchmark,\n168\t    horizontal reference at G=0.90'\n169\t  - 'Fig 7: Allocation efficiency surface — 3D plot of items × prompts × projected\n170\t    G, showing diminishing returns'\n171\t  analysis: 'Sensitivity analysis: bootstrap resample variance components 1000×, compute\n172\t    D-study projections for each → 95% CI on ''items needed for G≥0.90'' is [420,\n173\t    680] for MMLU。Key message shift: ''tripling items is 3-5× more effective than\n174\t    tripling prompts'' (down from ''10×'' in MVP)。仍然是strong practical recommendation但headline数字需要honest\n175\t    reporting。'\n176\t- name: 14B Model Extension\n177\t  plan_id: exp-005\n178\t  description: Qwen3-14B and DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B at BF16/FP16 on MMLU 200 items\n179\t  expected_results:\n180\t    qwen3_14b_item_id_share: 0.46\n181\t    deepseek_r1_14b_item_id_share: 0.34\n182\t    qwen3_14b_precision_main: 0.005\n183\t    deepseek_r1_precision_main: 0.009\n184\t    deepseek_r1_temperature_x_item: 0.11\n185\t    scale_effect_on_variance_structure: mixed — dense 14B similar to 8B; reasoning-distilled\n186\t      shows more temperature sensitivity\n187\t    g_qwen3_14b_current: 0.47\n188\t    g_deepseek_r1_14b_current: 0.38\n189\t  result_rationale: Qwen3-14B作为dense model的scale-up版本，variance structure与8B系列相似但item_id略高(0.46)——更大模型对easy\n190\t    items更consistently correct，使item difficulty gradient更极端，增加item方差贡献。DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B是reasoning-distilled模型，其CoT倾向使temperature\n191\t    sensitivity大幅升高（temperature×item=0.11），类似GSM8K的pattern——即使在MC task(MMLU)上，该模型也倾向于'思考'，引入generation-like\n192\t    variance。这导致其item_id share仅0.34。这个发现支持'variance structure depends on model architecture,\n193\t    not just task type'的扩展论点。Precision main effect在两个14B模型上都很小（dense model更稳定），验证separability在larger\n194\t    scale也成立。\n195\t  tables:\n196\t  - 'Table 11: 8B vs 14B variance component comparison — paired by architecture family'\n197\t  figures_description:\n198\t  - 'Fig 8: Paired bar chart — 8B vs 14B models showing variance structure shift with\n199\t    scale'\n200\t  analysis: 'Key finding: variance structure is jointly determined by task type AND\n201\t    model architecture (specifically, reasoning-distilled models behave like ''reasoning\n202\t    tasks'' even on MC benchmarks)。This adds nuance to the benchmark-dependent story。'\n203\t- name: Fine-Tuning G-Study\n204\t  plan_id: exp-006\n205\t  description: 'RoBERTa-large on SST-2/MNLI/RTE/COPA: 6 seeds × 3 LR × 4 shuffles\n206\t    × 3 GPUs × 2 precisions = 432 runs/task'\n207\t  expected_results:\n208\t    total_runs: 1728\n209\t    sst2_seed_variance_share: 0.18\n210\t    sst2_lr_variance_share: 0.31\n211\t    sst2_item_id_variance_share: 0.22\n212\t    sst2_shuffle_variance_share: 0.04\n213\t    sst2_gpu_variance_share: 0.003\n214\t    sst2_precision_variance_share: 0.006\n215\t    sst2_residual: 0.15\n216\t    sst2_seed_x_item: 0.05\n217\t    rte_seed_variance_share: 0.28\n218\t    rte_lr_variance_share: 0.22\n219\t    rte_item_id_variance_share: 0.19\n220\t    rte_seed_x_lr: 0.08\n221\t    copa_instability: high residual (0.32) due to small dataset size\n222\t    g_finetuning_current_practice: 0.52\n223\t    g_finetuning_5seeds_3lr: 0.78\n224\t    infra_method_separability_finetuning: holds — GPU×seed interaction < 0.5%\n225\t  result_rationale: Fine-tuning的variance hierarchy与inference根本不同：learning rate成为dominant\n226\t    factor(SST-2上0.31)因为它决定了optimization trajectory的convergence point——不同LR到达不同local\n227\t    minima。Seed是第二大因素(0.18-0.28)因为weight initialization和data ordering jointly决定optimization\n228\t    path。Item_id仍significant(0.19-0.22)但不再dominant——fine-tuning使模型适配了task distribution，减少了item\n229\t    difficulty的极端效应。RTE上seed更重要(0.28)因为小数据集(2.5k training)使optimization对initialization更敏感。GPU\n230\t    variance几乎为零(0.003)因为fine-tuning的随机性远大于hardware-level numerical差异。Data shuffle仅0.04——远低于预期，因为在足够epochs后shuffle的影响被averaged\n231\t    out。核心对比：inference中item dominates(43%)，fine-tuning中LR+seed dominates(~50%)——practitioners需要不同的reproducibility\n232\t    strategies for不同scenarios。\n233\t  tables:\n234\t  - 'Table 12: Fine-tuning variance decomposition — 4 tasks × top-8 components'\n235\t  - 'Table 13: Inference vs fine-tuning variance hierarchy comparison'\n236\t  - 'Table 14: Fine-tuning D-study — seeds and LR needed for G≥0.90'\n237\t  figures_description:\n238\t  - 'Fig 9: Side-by-side stacked bars — inference (MMLU) vs fine-tuning (SST-2) variance\n239\t    decomposition showing inverted hierarchy'\n240\t  - 'Fig 10: Fine-tuning D-study curves — G vs number of seeds, with LR as parameter'\n241\t  analysis: The inverted hierarchy (item→LR+seed) between inference and fine-tuning\n242\t    is a clean novel finding。It explains why the 'run 5 seeds and report mean±std'\n243\t    convention exists for fine-tuning but not for inference evaluation。Separability\n244\t    still holds in fine-tuning (GPU×methodology < 0.5%)，strengthening the cross-scenario\n245\t    generalization of Claim 2。\n246\t- name: LayerCast Precision Study\n247\t  plan_id: exp-007\n248\t  description: LayerCast (FP16 storage + FP32 compute) as 4th precision condition\n249\t    on MMLU\n250\t  expected_results:\n251\t    layercast_accuracy: 0.68\n252\t    layercast_vs_fp32_accuracy_diff: 0.006\n253\t    layercast_vs_bf16_accuracy_diff: -0.001\n254\t    layercast_item_level_agreement_with_bf16: 0.94\n255\t    precision_variance_with_layercast: 0.005\n256\t    precision_x_item_with_layercast: 0.022\n257\t    variance_reduction_vs_mixed_precision: 15-20% reduction in precision-related variance\n258\t      components\n259\t  result_rationale: LayerCast在FP16存储但FP32计算，理论上获得FP32的numerical stability同时保持FP16的memory\n260\t    efficiency。实测accuracy接近BF16/FP16（差异<0.1pp）而非接近pure FP32——这是因为在confound-fixed设置下FP32本身的accuracy已与BF16接近（差异<1pp），LayerCast的'storage\n261\t    in FP16'引入的quantization noise在compute阶段被FP32 accumulation部分补偿。Precision×item interaction从0.032降至0.022——LayerCast减少了precision-sensitive\n262\t    items上的variance但未完全消除（weight quantization仍在）。整体precision-related variance减少约18%，是有意义但非revolutionary的改进。作为mitigation\n263\t    strategy，值得在discussion中推荐但不足以成为standalone contribution。\n264\t  tables:\n265\t  - 'Table 15: 4-level precision comparison (FP32/BF16/FP16/LayerCast) — accuracy\n266\t    and variance metrics'\n267\t  figures_description:\n268\t  - 'Fig 11: Item-level accuracy correlation matrix across 4 precision conditions\n269\t    — showing LayerCast clusters with BF16/FP16'\n270\t  analysis: 'LayerCast provides modest variance reduction but does not fundamentally\n271\t    change the story。Best framed as a practical recommendation in discussion: ''for\n272\t    practitioners concerned about precision effects, LayerCast offers ~18% reduction\n273\t    in precision-related variance at minimal memory cost''。'\n274\t- name: MATH Benchmark Extension\n275\t  plan_id: exp-008\n276\t  description: MATH 200 items — reasoning-heavy with CoT, testing item dominance in\n277\t    extended generation\n278\t  expected_results:\n279\t    math_item_id_share: 0.22\n280\t    math_temperature_main: 0.04\n281\t    math_temperature_x_item: 0.15\n282\t    math_seed_x_item: 0.11\n283\t    math_residual: 0.3\n284\t    math_prompt_x_item: 0.06\n285\t    math_precision_main: 0.005\n286\t    math_g_current: 0.3\n287\t    math_items_for_g90: 950\n288\t    item_dominance_holds: false\n289\t    separability_holds: true\n290\t  result_rationale: MATH是最极端的reasoning benchmark——每题需要多步推导，CoT generation在每一步都受temperature和seed影响。Temperature×item=0.15因为难题（Level\n291\t    4-5）的solution path高度branching——temperature=0.7时可能走对路径，temperature=0.3时可能卡在suboptimal\n292\t    path。Seed×item=0.11同理——不同seed改变了sampling trajectory。Residual高达0.30反映了即使fixed所有facets，generation的intrinsic\n293\t    stochasticity（vLLM default设置下的CUDA nondeterminism在long generation中累积）。Item_id仅0.22——仍然是single\n294\t    largest individual component但margin很小（vs residual 0.30如果将residual视为'unexplained'则item不是最大）。这是median档下GSM8K\n295\t    pattern的加强版，确认了'reasoning tasks have fundamentally different variance structure'。Separability仍成立（precision/hardware与methodology无direct\n296\t    interaction>1%）——numerical precision不影响推理策略选择，只影响individual token probabilities。G=0.30极低，需要~950\n297\t    items才能达到G≥0.90——对MATH这样仅500题的benchmark来说实质上不可达，这本身是对当前evaluation practice的strong\n298\t    critique。\n299\t  tables:\n300\t  - 'Table 16: MATH vs GSM8K vs MMLU variance structure comparison — progression from\n301\t    MC to light-reasoning to heavy-reasoning'\n302\t  figures_description:\n303\t  - 'Fig 12: Variance structure gradient plot — x-axis = ''reasoning intensity'' (MMLU→ARC→GSM8K→MATH),\n304\t    y-axis = component shares, showing smooth transition from item-dominated to residual/interaction-dominated'\n305\t  analysis: 'MATH completes the picture: variance structure is a continuous function\n306\t    of task reasoning demands。For MC knowledge tasks, item selection is the key lever;\n307\t    for reasoning tasks, temperature control and massive item sampling are both needed。The\n308\t    ''one-size-fits-all'' reproducibility budget is insufficient — practitioners need\n309\t    task-type-specific guidance。This benchmark-dependency finding becomes the paper''s\n310\t    central novel contribution under the median tier narrative。'\n311\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01RdahfyKVtwMwAfTJrn7pUJ","session_id":"e13969ce-5d2d-4a82-903b-b87fae4e296f","uuid":"06699a28-90e3-4f33-9b36-60dae5024cba","timestamp":"2026-05-20T23:32:30.886Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_017BU5wU5Un7no1eN5uaXZPS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019SUCnKsMZRwRnbBhjN2qc8","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus areas are **novelty**, **related work positioning**, and **significance/impact**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Reproducibility Budgets for LLM Evaluation: A Generalizability Theory Analysis of Variance Sources Across Benchmarks, Models, and Evaluation Metrics\"\n\nThe paper applies Generalizability Theory (G-theory) to LLM evaluation, conducting a multi-benchmark crossed-facet variance decomposition across five benchmarks (MMLU, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag, GSM8K, MATH), six models (4×7-9B: Qwen3-8B, Llama-3.1-8B, Gemma-2-9B-IT, Mistral-7B-v0.3; 2×14B: Qwen3-14B, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B; plus RoBERTa-large for fine-tuning), and over 4 million evaluation records. Three facet classes are crossed: measurement (item_id), methodology (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering), infrastructure (precision, GPU hardware).\n\nThree main claims:\n1. **Cross-benchmark variance gradient**: item_id share decreases monotonically from 56% (MMLU) to 21% (MATH), a 35pp gradient. CoT stochasticity shifts variance into temp×item and seed×item on reasoning tasks.\n2. **Confirmatory separability null**: direct infra×methodology <0.3%, validating isolated-factor studies at aggregate level. Both factor types interact substantially with items (~3.3% infra×item, ~16% method×item).\n3. **D-study protocol reversal**: MMLU is item-limited (420 items for G≥0.90), GSM8K is seed-limited (10 seeds). Current practice G<0.25 everywhere — below any measurement discipline's minimum.\n\n## Related Work cited in the paper:\n- **Variance sources in LLM eval**: Sclar et al. [18] (prompt sensitivity, 76% swing), Pezeshkpour & Hruschka [14] and Lu et al. [9] (option ordering 5-13%), Yuan et al. [22] (hardware/precision 9%), Biderman et al. [1] (reproducibility failures catalog). Paper claims: each prior study isolates 1-2 factors on a fixed benchmark; none crosses infra with methodology or compares across benchmarks.\n- **Variance decomposition in ML**: Bouthillier et al. [2] (ANOVA for ML benchmarks, JMLR 2021), Henderson et al. [6] (deep RL), Dodge et al. [5] and Sellam et al. [19] (fine-tuning instability), Dehghani et al. [4] (benchmark lottery). Paper claims: these use ANOVA/bootstrap but not G-theory, thus lack D-study machinery.\n- **G-theory in NLP**: Mesting [11] (G-theory on single benchmark, 5 methodology facets, item_id 43.8%). Paper claims 4 differences: (a) crossing infra with methodology, (b) 5 benchmarks, (c) model as crossed facet, (d) metric-conditional budgets.\n- **IRT in NLP**: Lalor et al. [8], Rodriguez et al. [17], Polo et al. [16] (tinyBenchmarks), Perlitz et al. [13]. Paper positions IRT and G-theory as complementary.\n- **Other**: Hua et al. [7] (generation-based evaluation artifacts), Messina & Scotta [10] (floating-point non-associativity).\n\n## Paper Structure (14 pages):\n1. Abstract + Introduction (pp. 1-2): problem framing, 3 contributions\n2. Related Work (p. 2-3): 4 paragraphs covering prior variance studies, ANOVA decomposition, G-theory, IRT\n3. Method (pp. 3-4): G-theory formulation, facet structure, models/benchmarks, infrastructure controls\n4. MMLU Full-Scale G-Study (pp. 4-5): variance decomposition, engine confound correction, cross-model consistency, item resampling stability\n5. Cross-Benchmark Variance Gradient (pp. 5-6): 4-benchmark comparison, MATH extension, GSM8K per-model\n6. Multi-Hardware Separability Test (pp. 7): 2 GPU types crossed design\n7. Comprehensive Analysis (pp. 7-8): model-as-facet, bootstrap CIs, D-study projections\n8. Robustness and Extensions (pp. 9-10): 14B scale, fine-tuning G-study, LayerCast\n9. Discussion (pp. 10-11): cross-benchmark gradient as unifying explanation, \"no universal recipe\" implications, current practice inadequacy, separability nuance, model-as-facet, limitations\n10. Conclusion (p. 12)\n11. References (pp. 12-13): 22 references\n12. Appendices A-C (p. 14): MATH per-model, logprob scoring, higher-order interactions\n\n## Your Review Task\nProduce a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel are the three claims individually and collectively? Is the application of G-theory to multi-benchmark LLM evaluation genuinely new, or incremental over Mesting [11]?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are there important missing references? Is the positioning against prior work fair and accurate?\n3. **Significance and impact**: Will this paper change how people evaluate LLMs? Are the D-study budgets practically useful? Is the \"no universal recipe\" finding surprising or obvious?\n4. **Framing and narrative**: Is the paper well-written? Are the claims well-supported? Is the discussion balanced?\n5. **Scope concerns**: Is 5 benchmarks × 6 models × 7-14B sufficient to support the generality of claims? What about larger models (70B+), proprietary APIs, non-English benchmarks?\n6. **Strengths and weaknesses** (bulleted lists)\n7. **Questions for authors** (numbered)\n8. **Minor issues**\n9. **Score** (1-6) with confidence (low/medium/high)\n\nBe thorough and critical but fair. Report in structured markdown."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8919,"cache_read_input_tokens":56340,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8919},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e13969ce-5d2d-4a82-903b-b87fae4e296f","uuid":"77e1e032-27c3-4195-8c01-2365ddc6b728"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a7552e4415d46b4e3","tool_use_id":"toolu_019SUCnKsMZRwRnbBhjN2qc8","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus areas are **novelty**, **related work positioning**, and **significance/impact**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Reproducibility Budgets for LLM Evaluation: A Generalizability Theory Analysis of Variance Sources Across Benchmarks, Models, and Evaluation Metrics\"\n\nThe paper applies Generalizability Theory (G-theory) to LLM evaluation, conducting a multi-benchmark crossed-facet variance decomposition across five benchmarks (MMLU, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag, GSM8K, MATH), six models (4×7-9B: Qwen3-8B, Llama-3.1-8B, Gemma-2-9B-IT, Mistral-7B-v0.3; 2×14B: Qwen3-14B, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B; plus RoBERTa-large for fine-tuning), and over 4 million evaluation records. Three facet classes are crossed: measurement (item_id), methodology (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering), infrastructure (precision, GPU hardware).\n\nThree main claims:\n1. **Cross-benchmark variance gradient**: item_id share decreases monotonically from 56% (MMLU) to 21% (MATH), a 35pp gradient. CoT stochasticity shifts variance into temp×item and seed×item on reasoning tasks.\n2. **Confirmatory separability null**: direct infra×methodology <0.3%, validating isolated-factor studies at aggregate level. Both factor types interact substantially with items (~3.3% infra×item, ~16% method×item).\n3. **D-study protocol reversal**: MMLU is item-limited (420 items for G≥0.90), GSM8K is seed-limited (10 seeds). Current practice G<0.25 everywhere — below any measurement discipline's minimum.\n\n## Related Work cited in the paper:\n- **Variance sources in LLM eval**: Sclar et al. [18] (prompt sensitivity, 76% swing), Pezeshkpour & Hruschka [14] and Lu et al. [9] (option ordering 5-13%), Yuan et al. [22] (hardware/precision 9%), Biderman et al. [1] (reproducibility failures catalog). Paper claims: each prior study isolates 1-2 factors on a fixed benchmark; none crosses infra with methodology or compares across benchmarks.\n- **Variance decomposition in ML**: Bouthillier et al. [2] (ANOVA for ML benchmarks, JMLR 2021), Henderson et al. [6] (deep RL), Dodge et al. [5] and Sellam et al. [19] (fine-tuning instability), Dehghani et al. [4] (benchmark lottery). Paper claims: these use ANOVA/bootstrap but not G-theory, thus lack D-study machinery.\n- **G-theory in NLP**: Mesting [11] (G-theory on single benchmark, 5 methodology facets, item_id 43.8%). Paper claims 4 differences: (a) crossing infra with methodology, (b) 5 benchmarks, (c) model as crossed facet, (d) metric-conditional budgets.\n- **IRT in NLP**: Lalor et al. [8], Rodriguez et al. [17], Polo et al. [16] (tinyBenchmarks), Perlitz et al. [13]. Paper positions IRT and G-theory as complementary.\n- **Other**: Hua et al. [7] (generation-based evaluation artifacts), Messina & Scotta [10] (floating-point non-associativity).\n\n## Paper Structure (14 pages):\n1. Abstract + Introduction (pp. 1-2): problem framing, 3 contributions\n2. Related Work (p. 2-3): 4 paragraphs covering prior variance studies, ANOVA decomposition, G-theory, IRT\n3. Method (pp. 3-4): G-theory formulation, facet structure, models/benchmarks, infrastructure controls\n4. MMLU Full-Scale G-Study (pp. 4-5): variance decomposition, engine confound correction, cross-model consistency, item resampling stability\n5. Cross-Benchmark Variance Gradient (pp. 5-6): 4-benchmark comparison, MATH extension, GSM8K per-model\n6. Multi-Hardware Separability Test (pp. 7): 2 GPU types crossed design\n7. Comprehensive Analysis (pp. 7-8): model-as-facet, bootstrap CIs, D-study projections\n8. Robustness and Extensions (pp. 9-10): 14B scale, fine-tuning G-study, LayerCast\n9. Discussion (pp. 10-11): cross-benchmark gradient as unifying explanation, \"no universal recipe\" implications, current practice inadequacy, separability nuance, model-as-facet, limitations\n10. Conclusion (p. 12)\n11. References (pp. 12-13): 22 references\n12. Appendices A-C (p. 14): MATH per-model, logprob scoring, higher-order interactions\n\n## Your Review Task\nProduce a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel are the three claims individually and collectively? Is the application of G-theory to multi-benchmark LLM evaluation genuinely new, or incremental over Mesting [11]?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are there important missing references? Is the positioning against prior work fair and accurate?\n3. **Significance and impact**: Will this paper change how people evaluate LLMs? Are the D-study budgets practically useful? Is the \"no universal recipe\" finding surprising or obvious?\n4. **Framing and narrative**: Is the paper well-written? Are the claims well-supported? Is the discussion balanced?\n5. **Scope concerns**: Is 5 benchmarks × 6 models × 7-14B sufficient to support the generality of claims? What about larger models (70B+), proprietary APIs, non-English benchmarks?\n6. **Strengths and weaknesses** (bulleted lists)\n7. **Questions for authors** (numbered)\n8. **Minor issues**\n9. **Score** (1-6) with confidence (low/medium/high)\n\nBe thorough and critical but fair. Report in structured markdown.","uuid":"dd24411d-9eb3-43c7-9056-a96037ec5f44","session_id":"e13969ce-5d2d-4a82-903b-b87fae4e296f"}
