{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019SUCnKsMZRwRnbBhjN2qc8","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted successfully.\n\n**Summary of the R2 review for the \"best\" tier of dream_20260520T222113:**\n\n**Score: 4/6 (Weak Accept)** | Confidence: High\n\n**Key assessments:**\n\n- **Novelty**: The cross-benchmark variance gradient (56% to 21% item_id share across 5 benchmarks) is the paper's strongest and genuinely novel contribution -- it unifies contradictory prior findings. The separability null is confirmatory, not novel. The D-study protocol reversal has moderate novelty but high practical value. Collectively, the three claims are stronger than individually.\n\n- **Related work**: Substantially improved from the earlier 7-reference draft to 22 references with proper positioning. Still missing GeneralizIT (2024), Kunievsky (2025), Brittlebench (2026), Bui (2025), and ThinkingMachines (2025) -- all present in the project's own literature map but absent from the paper.\n\n- **Significance**: The D-study budgets are immediately actionable. The \"G < 0.25 everywhere\" finding is an important indictment of current practice. Impact depends on community adoption of G-theory as a reliability framework.\n\n- **Primary weaknesses**: (1) Zero figures is a serious presentation gap for a variance decomposition paper; (2) infrastructure crossing yields near-null results despite being the claimed advance over Mesting; (3) title promises \"Evaluation Metrics\" but delivers only binary scoring; (4) scope limited to 7-14B open-weight models.\n\nThe review is saved at `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519/projects/nlp_variance_gtheory/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T222113/best/review_report.md`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a7552e4415d46b4e3 (use SendMessage with to: 'a7552e4415d46b4e3' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 64377\ntool_uses: 16\nduration_ms: 207490</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e13969ce-5d2d-4a82-903b-b87fae4e296f","uuid":"032d9e80-f56c-4a44-ad12-545bed067c8f","timestamp":"2026-05-20T23:35:59.144Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus areas are **novelty**, **related work positioning**, and **significance/impact**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Reproducibility Budgets for LLM Evaluation: A Generalizability Theory Analysis of Variance Sources Across Benchmarks, Models, and Evaluation Metrics\"\n\nThe paper applies Generalizability Theory (G-theory) to LLM evaluation, conducting a multi-benchmark crossed-facet variance decomposition across five benchmarks (MMLU, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag, GSM8K, MATH), six models (4×7-9B: Qwen3-8B, Llama-3.1-8B, Gemma-2-9B-IT, Mistral-7B-v0.3; 2×14B: Qwen3-14B, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B; plus RoBERTa-large for fine-tuning), and over 4 million evaluation records. Three facet classes are crossed: measurement (item_id), methodology (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering), infrastructure (precision, GPU hardware).\n\nThree main claims:\n1. **Cross-benchmark variance gradient**: item_id share decreases monotonically from 56% (MMLU) to 21% (MATH), a 35pp gradient. CoT stochasticity shifts variance into temp×item and seed×item on reasoning tasks.\n2. **Confirmatory separability null**: direct infra×methodology <0.3%, validating isolated-factor studies at aggregate level. Both factor types interact substantially with items (~3.3% infra×item, ~16% method×item).\n3. **D-study protocol reversal**: MMLU is item-limited (420 items for G≥0.90), GSM8K is seed-limited (10 seeds). Current practice G<0.25 everywhere — below any measurement discipline's minimum.\n\n## Related Work cited in the paper:\n- **Variance sources in LLM eval**: Sclar et al. [18] (prompt sensitivity, 76% swing), Pezeshkpour & Hruschka [14] and Lu et al. [9] (option ordering 5-13%), Yuan et al. [22] (hardware/precision 9%), Biderman et al. [1] (reproducibility failures catalog). Paper claims: each prior study isolates 1-2 factors on a fixed benchmark; none crosses infra with methodology or compares across benchmarks.\n- **Variance decomposition in ML**: Bouthillier et al. [2] (ANOVA for ML benchmarks, JMLR 2021), Henderson et al. [6] (deep RL), Dodge et al. [5] and Sellam et al. [19] (fine-tuning instability), Dehghani et al. [4] (benchmark lottery). Paper claims: these use ANOVA/bootstrap but not G-theory, thus lack D-study machinery.\n- **G-theory in NLP**: Mesting [11] (G-theory on single benchmark, 5 methodology facets, item_id 43.8%). Paper claims 4 differences: (a) crossing infra with methodology, (b) 5 benchmarks, (c) model as crossed facet, (d) metric-conditional budgets.\n- **IRT in NLP**: Lalor et al. [8], Rodriguez et al. [17], Polo et al. [16] (tinyBenchmarks), Perlitz et al. [13]. Paper positions IRT and G-theory as complementary.\n- **Other**: Hua et al. [7] (generation-based evaluation artifacts), Messina & Scotta [10] (floating-point non-associativity).\n\n## Paper Structure (14 pages):\n1. Abstract + Introduction (pp. 1-2): problem framing, 3 contributions\n2. Related Work (p. 2-3): 4 paragraphs covering prior variance studies, ANOVA decomposition, G-theory, IRT\n3. Method (pp. 3-4): G-theory formulation, facet structure, models/benchmarks, infrastructure controls\n4. MMLU Full-Scale G-Study (pp. 4-5): variance decomposition, engine confound correction, cross-model consistency, item resampling stability\n5. Cross-Benchmark Variance Gradient (pp. 5-6): 4-benchmark comparison, MATH extension, GSM8K per-model\n6. Multi-Hardware Separability Test (pp. 7): 2 GPU types crossed design\n7. Comprehensive Analysis (pp. 7-8): model-as-facet, bootstrap CIs, D-study projections\n8. Robustness and Extensions (pp. 9-10): 14B scale, fine-tuning G-study, LayerCast\n9. Discussion (pp. 10-11): cross-benchmark gradient as unifying explanation, \"no universal recipe\" implications, current practice inadequacy, separability nuance, model-as-facet, limitations\n10. Conclusion (p. 12)\n11. References (pp. 12-13): 22 references\n12. Appendices A-C (p. 14): MATH per-model, logprob scoring, higher-order interactions\n\n## Your Review Task\nProduce a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel are the three claims individually and collectively? Is the application of G-theory to multi-benchmark LLM evaluation genuinely new, or incremental over Mesting [11]?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are there important missing references? Is the positioning against prior work fair and accurate?\n3. **Significance and impact**: Will this paper change how people evaluate LLMs? Are the D-study budgets practically useful? Is the \"no universal recipe\" finding surprising or obvious?\n4. **Framing and narrative**: Is the paper well-written? Are the claims well-supported? Is the discussion balanced?\n5. **Scope concerns**: Is 5 benchmarks × 6 models × 7-14B sufficient to support the generality of claims? What about larger models (70B+), proprietary APIs, non-English benchmarks?\n6. **Strengths and weaknesses** (bulleted lists)\n7. **Questions for authors** (numbered)\n8. **Minor issues**\n9. **Score** (1-6) with confidence (low/medium/high)\n\nBe thorough and critical but fair. Report in structured markdown.","agentId":"a7552e4415d46b4e3","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted successfully.\n\n**Summary of the R2 review for the \"best\" tier of dream_20260520T222113:**\n\n**Score: 4/6 (Weak Accept)** | Confidence: High\n\n**Key assessments:**\n\n- **Novelty**: The cross-benchmark variance gradient (56% to 21% item_id share across 5 benchmarks) is the paper's strongest and genuinely novel contribution -- it unifies contradictory prior findings. The separability null is confirmatory, not novel. The D-study protocol reversal has moderate novelty but high practical value. Collectively, the three claims are stronger than individually.\n\n- **Related work**: Substantially improved from the earlier 7-reference draft to 22 references with proper positioning. Still missing GeneralizIT (2024), Kunievsky (2025), Brittlebench (2026), Bui (2025), and ThinkingMachines (2025) -- all present in the project's own literature map but absent from the paper.\n\n- **Significance**: The D-study budgets are immediately actionable. The \"G < 0.25 everywhere\" finding is an important indictment of current practice. Impact depends on community adoption of G-theory as a reliability framework.\n\n- **Primary weaknesses**: (1) Zero figures is a serious presentation gap for a variance decomposition paper; (2) infrastructure crossing yields near-null results despite being the claimed advance over Mesting; (3) title promises \"Evaluation Metrics\" but delivers only binary scoring; (4) scope limited to 7-14B open-weight models.\n\nThe review is saved at `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519/projects/nlp_variance_gtheory/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T222113/best/review_report.md`."}],"totalDurationMs":207490,"totalTokens":64377,"totalToolUseCount":16,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6371,"cache_read_input_tokens":57585,"output_tokens":420,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6371},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":420,"cache_read_input_tokens":57585,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6371,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6371,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":7,"searchCount":4,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":5}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a1382c0317ef91356","tool_use_id":"toolu_01RdahfyKVtwMwAfTJrn7pUJ","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":91517,"tool_uses":26,"duration_ms":252856},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"51a8525c-9ccc-4e78-affc-dae1031acf9d","session_id":"e13969ce-5d2d-4a82-903b-b87fae4e296f"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018UZjboETjYWRDNLDGxzk11","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01ANkaLG88sLHHJ96ZcYnU6f","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"---\ndream_id: dream_20260520T222113\ntier: median\noverall_score: weak accept\ntimestamp: 2026-05-20T23:45:00+00:00\n---\n\n# Dream Review Report — R1 (Methodology & Experimental Rigor)\n\n**Paper:** Reproducibility Budgets for LLM Evaluation: A Generalizability Theory Analysis of Variance Sources Across Benchmarks, Models, and Evaluation Metrics\n\n**Tier:** Median\n\n**Review mode:** R1 focus — Methodology, Experimental Design, Statistical Rigor\n\n---\n\n## 1. Paper Summary\n\nThe paper applies Generalizability Theory (G-theory) to LLM evaluation, conducting multi-benchmark crossed-facet variance decompositions across five benchmarks (MMLU, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag, GSM8K, MATH), six models (4x7-9B + 2x14B), two GPU architectures, and multiple levels of precision, prompt template, seed, temperature, and answer ordering. Over 3 million evaluation records are analyzed. Three claims are made: (1) a benchmark-conditional variance gradient where item dominance ranges from 48% (MMLU) to 28% (MATH) under model-as-facet analysis; (2) infrastructure x methodology separability at the aggregate level (<0.5% on knowledge benchmarks, rising to 3% on math-generation benchmarks); (3) metric-conditional, benchmark-specific reproducibility budgets via D-study projections. Extensions cover 14B models, RoBERTa fine-tuning, LayerCast precision mitigation, and three scoring metrics.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Methodological Soundness\n\n### 2.1 G-Theory Framework Application\n\n**Largely correct, with one significant improvement over Dream #1.** The revised paper treats model as a crossed random facet (Section 4.4 / Table 5), which was the most critical methodological concern in the prior review. This properly separates model×item interaction (16-22%) from pure item variance and residual. The 4pp systematic reduction across all benchmarks is plausible and consistent with psychometric expectation when objects of measurement have heterogeneous difficulty profiles.\n\n**Henderson Method III vs. REML.** The paper uses Henderson Method III (expected mean squares) as primary estimation with REML as sensitivity check. This is defensible: Henderson III is unbiased for balanced designs, and the design is nearly balanced (fully crossed within each experiment). The reported max divergence of 2.1pp with preserved rank ordering is reassuring. However:\n\n- **Concern:** Henderson Method III can produce negative variance component estimates for near-zero components. The MATH table (Table 22 in the imagined experiments) shows a negative \"other\" entry (-1.5%), which is characteristic of this issue. The paper should explicitly state how negative estimates are handled (set to zero? redistributed? left as-is?). This matters for the separability claim where infra×method components are near zero — a negative estimate truncated to zero looks different from a genuinely zero estimate.\n\n- **Concern:** The G-theory formula presented in Equation 2 is simplified. The actual D-study formula for a multi-facet crossed design with 5+ facets involves many more terms. The paper should either present the full formula used or cite a specific implementation (e.g., GeneralizIT package). The simplified formula risks misleading readers about how D-study projections were actually computed.\n\n### 2.2 Crossed Random-Effects Assumptions\n\n**Items as random facet:** Justified. The 200 items per benchmark are sampled (stratified) from larger pools (MMLU: 14,042; ARC: 2,590; GSM8K: 1,319; HellaSwag: 10,042; MATH: 5,000). The random-facet assumption requires that observed items are exchangeable draws from a universe of admissible observations. Stratified sampling strengthens this by ensuring subject/topic coverage.\n\n**Model as random facet:** Partially justified. Four 7-9B models from different families (Qwen, Llama, Gemma, Mistral) are treated as exchangeable draws from the universe of \"7-9B instruction-tuned LLMs.\" This is defensible but arguable — the model universe is not well-defined, and four models provide limited degrees of freedom for estimating model-related variance components. The 14B extension (2 models) is even more constrained. The paper should acknowledge that model-as-facet with k=4 yields imprecise estimates of model variance components and that the model universe is narrowly defined (open-weight, instruction-tuned, 7-9B).\n\n**Prompt as random facet:** The 6 prompt templates are described but their generation process matters for the random-effects assumption. If they were systematically designed to vary specific dimensions (e.g., formality, instruction explicitness), they may not represent random draws from a prompt universe. The paper should clarify whether prompt templates were generated to be representative (supporting random effects) or to maximize diversity (which biases variance estimates upward).\n\n**Seed as random facet:** Clearly justified — seeds are arbitrary integers drawn from a large space.\n\n**Temperature as random facet:** Problematic. With only 2-3 levels (e.g., 0, 0.5, 1.0), treating temperature as a random facet is statistically dubious — these are specific points chosen to span the parameter range, not random draws from a temperature distribution. Temperature is better modeled as fixed. If treated as random, the variance estimates carry extreme uncertainty with 2-3 df. The paper should address this.\n\n**Ordering as random facet:** Four orderings (ABCD, BCDA, CDAB, DABC) — these are systematic rotations, not random draws. The random-effects assumption is defensible only if one argues these represent exchangeable draws from the universe of possible orderings, which is reasonable for 4-option MC but should be noted.\n\n### 2.3 Variance Estimation for Binary Data\n\n**Critical methodological question.** Binary correct (0/1) data violates the normality assumption underlying standard G-theory variance component estimation. Henderson Method III and REML both assume continuous, approximately normal response variables. For binary data:\n\n- Variance components are bounded by the Bernoulli variance p(1-p), creating ceiling effects for items near p=0 or p=1.\n- Item-level variance is mechanically linked to item difficulty — easy and hard items contribute less variance by construction, inflating the apparent dominance of \"mid-difficulty\" items.\n- The paper's finding that item_id is the largest variance source is partially an artifact of using binary scoring on items with heterogeneous difficulty. This is the well-known \"item difficulty confound\" in classical test theory.\n\nThe text-exact-match metric (also binary) and logprob metric (continuous) provide useful sensitivity checks. The logprob result (item_id = 55%) is methodologically cleaner because the response variable is continuous, avoiding the binary data issue. The paper should discuss the binary data limitation explicitly and note that logprob scoring provides the most methodologically sound variance decomposition.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Experimental Design\n\n### 3.1 Factorial Design Adequacy\n\n**MMLU (Exp 1): Strong.** 4 models × 3 precisions × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 3 temperatures × 4 orderings × 200 items = 1,036,800 observations. This is the richest design and supports all claimed decompositions. The V0 enforce_eager control eliminates the engine confound — this is excellent experimental hygiene.\n\n**Cross-benchmark (Exp 3): Adequate but reduced.** 2 precisions × 2 temperatures (vs. 3×3 on MMLU). The reduction is justified by compute constraints but creates an asymmetry: FP32 is absent from cross-benchmark experiments, meaning the precision facet is restricted to BF16/FP16 (which differ minimally). The claimed cross-benchmark gradient in precision effects (MMLU 3.0% vs. GSM8K 1.0%) is confounded with the different precision levels tested. This should be explicitly acknowledged.\n\n**Multi-hardware (Exp 4): Adequate.** 2 GPU types is the minimum for estimating hardware effects. With 1 df for hardware main effect, the estimate is necessarily imprecise. The paper correctly frames hardware findings as directional rather than definitive.\n\n**Item resampling (Exp 2): Well-designed.** Three independent stratified draws with different seeds. The CV=0.19 (paper) or CV=0.19 (imagined experiments) on item_id is an important honesty result. However, three draws provide only a rough estimate of resampling variability — 10+ draws would be more convincing, though the compute cost may be prohibitive.\n\n**Fine-tuning (Exp 7): Adequate for its scope.** 432 runs per task × 4 tasks = 1,728 total. The fully crossed design (seed × LR × shuffle × GPU × precision) is clean. The RoBERTa-large choice is acknowledged as a limitation (encoder-only, classification). This experiment is best framed as a conceptual extension rather than a direct generalization.\n\n### 3.2 Missing Controls and Confounds\n\n1. **Batch size / sequence length confound.** vLLM batches requests dynamically. If different benchmark items produce different output lengths (especially GSM8K/MATH with CoT), batch composition may vary, introducing an unmodeled variance source that gets absorbed into residual or item interactions. The paper uses \"default vLLM settings\" which is realistic but means batch-level nondeterminism is present.\n\n2. **Prompt template × benchmark interaction.** The same 6 prompt templates are used across benchmarks, but their appropriateness may vary by task. A prompt template that works well for MMLU factual recall may be poorly suited for GSM8K math reasoning. This could inflate prompt×item interactions on some benchmarks for reasons unrelated to the \"prompt sensitivity\" construct.\n\n3. **Item difficulty distribution differs across benchmarks.** MMLU items span a wide difficulty range; GSM8K items at 8B model scale cluster near 0.4-0.6 accuracy; MATH items are heavily bimodal (trivial or impossible for 8B models). These distributional differences mechanically affect how much variance is attributable to items vs. interactions. The paper's \"cognitive complexity\" explanation for the cross-benchmark gradient is plausible but not the only explanation — difficulty distribution differences could account for part of the pattern.\n\n4. **Stratified sampling details.** The paper mentions stratified sampling across 57 MMLU subjects, but the stratification scheme for other benchmarks (GSM8K difficulty levels? MATH topics?) is not described. If non-MMLU benchmarks lack stratification, their item samples may be less representative.\n\n5. **Answer extraction method.** For generation-based scoring, the method of extracting answers from generated text is a potential confound. Different regex/parsing strategies could produce different binary scores for the same generation. This is not discussed.\n\n### 3.3 Item Sampling Strategy\n\nThe 200-item stratified sample from MMLU's 14,042 items (1.4%) is a reasonable balance between compute cost and representativeness. The resampling stability test (CV=0.19) properly quantifies the sampling uncertainty. For other benchmarks:\n\n- GSM8K: 200/1,319 = 15.2% — much denser sampling, less sampling uncertainty expected.\n- ARC-Challenge: 200/2,590 = 7.7% — moderate.\n- HellaSwag: 200/10,042 = 2.0% — similar to MMLU.\n- MATH: 200/5,000 = 4.0% — moderate.\n\nThe paper should note that cross-benchmark comparisons of item_id variance share are confounded with sampling fraction — benchmarks with denser item sampling (GSM8K) mechanically have lower item-sampling uncertainty, which could affect the D-study projections.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Statistical Rigor\n\n### 4.1 Bootstrap Confidence Intervals\n\n**Construction:** 1,000 item×condition resamples. This is a standard nonparametric bootstrap for G-theory (cf. Brennan, 2001, Ch. 10). The resampling unit matters: resampling items treats items as the primary sampling unit, which is appropriate given that item selection is the largest variance source.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- The bootstrap resamples items but holds conditions (prompt, seed, temp, precision, ordering) fixed. This captures item-sampling uncertainty but not condition-sampling uncertainty. A more thorough approach would resample both items and conditions (double bootstrap), though this is computationally expensive and rarely done in G-theory applications. The paper should acknowledge this limitation.\n- The reported CI widths (~0.14 for item_id) are wide enough that the practical implications change substantially across the CI range. For example, MMLU item_id [0.41, 0.55] maps to different D-study recommendations at each endpoint. The paper handles this well by reporting D-study CIs ([350, 510] for G≥0.90 on MMLU).\n\n### 4.2 D-Study Projections\n\n**Validity:** D-study projections assume that variance components estimated from the G-study generalize to the projected design. This is the standard G-theory assumption and is reasonable when the projected design changes only the number of levels (e.g., more items, more seeds) rather than the nature of the facets. The projections in Table 8 change item counts while holding prompt/seed counts at \"optimal\" levels — this is valid.\n\n**Concern — optimal prompt/seed counts.** The D-study recommends \"3 prompts / 4 seeds\" for MMLU and \"4 prompts / 6 seeds\" for GSM8K. How are these \"optimal\" counts determined? If they are chosen to minimize total compute for a target G, the optimization procedure should be described. If they are chosen post-hoc to make the item counts look reasonable, this is problematic.\n\n**Concern — extrapolation to 420+ items.** The G-study uses 200 items. Projecting to 420 items assumes linear scaling of the error reduction, which holds under the random-sampling model. However, if the additional 220 items are drawn from the same pool, they may not be independent of the original 200 (especially for smaller pools like GSM8K with only 1,319 total items). The finite-population correction should be applied when the projected sample is a large fraction of the universe.\n\n### 4.3 REML Sensitivity Check\n\n**Sufficient for its purpose.** The 2.1pp maximum divergence with preserved rankings is adequate evidence that the choice between Henderson III and REML does not affect qualitative conclusions. However, REML is generally preferred for unbalanced designs. Since the within-experiment designs are balanced (fully crossed), Henderson III is appropriate. The sensitivity check confirms this.\n\n### 4.4 Permutation Test for Gradient\n\nThe p=0.003 for the gradient direction (MMLU > HellaSwag on item_id) is properly reported. The paper correctly notes that the CIs overlap partially at the margins ([0.38, 0.41]), supporting directional but not magnitude claims. This is honest and appropriate.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Reproducibility Concerns\n\n1. **vLLM version dependency.** The paper uses vLLM 0.9.2 with V0 enforce_eager. vLLM's behavior changes across versions (the V0/V1 engine confound demonstrates this). The results may not replicate on vLLM 0.8.x or 1.0.x. The paper should pin the exact vLLM commit hash, not just the version number.\n\n2. **Model weight provenance.** The paper lists model names but not specific weight versions (e.g., HuggingFace revision hashes). Model weights on HuggingFace can be updated without changing the model card name. For reproducibility, exact weight hashes are needed.\n\n3. **CUDA version and driver.** CUDA nondeterminism is a core topic of the paper. The exact CUDA version, cuDNN version, and driver version should be reported. The paper mentions \"CUDA 12.8\" support for vLLM 0.9.2 but does not pin the exact software stack.\n\n4. **Item sample seeds.** The stratified sampling seeds for the 200-item samples should be released. The resampling stability test uses seeds 0, 7, 99 — are these the only seeds tested? If the main experiments use a single seed (e.g., seed 0), the results are conditional on that particular draw.\n\n5. **Prompt template text.** The 6 prompt templates are critical to reproducibility but their exact text is not provided in the paper. They should appear in an appendix.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Strengths\n\n- **Comprehensive factorial design.** The MMLU experiment (1,036,800 observations, 5,184 conditions) is one of the largest crossed factorial designs in LLM evaluation research. The systematic approach — full crossing on MMLU, reduced crossing on other benchmarks, explicit confound controls — reflects careful experimental planning.\n\n- **Engine confound discovery and fix.** Identifying that default vLLM routes FP32 through a different engine (V1 CUDA graph) than BF16/FP16 (V0 eager) is a standalone methodological contribution. The enforce_eager fix is simple, actionable, and immediately useful to the community.\n\n- **Model-as-facet analysis.** This revision correctly treats model as a crossed random facet, addressing the most fundamental concern from Dream #1. The systematic 4pp reduction across all benchmarks and the extraction of 16-22% model×item interaction is a clean result.\n\n- **Multi-benchmark coverage.** Five benchmarks spanning knowledge retrieval (MMLU, ARC), commonsense reasoning (HellaSwag), arithmetic reasoning (GSM8K), and proof reasoning (MATH) provides genuine coverage of the LLM evaluation landscape. The three-tier clustering is more nuanced than a simple monotonic gradient.\n\n- **Metric-conditional analysis.** The binary/text-match/logprob comparison (Table 9) is a first-order finding: G ranges from 0.32 to 0.97 depending on the scoring metric. This alone justifies the paper — practitioners cannot discuss \"reliability\" without specifying the metric.\n\n- **Honest uncertainty quantification.** Bootstrap CIs on variance components, D-study CIs on item counts, resampling stability tests, and REML sensitivity checks collectively demonstrate statistical maturity. The paper acknowledges that the gradient is directional rather than precisely quantified.\n\n- **Three-tier narrative is stronger than two-cluster.** Including MATH extends the gradient from {MMLU, ARC} vs. {GSM8K, HellaSwag} to a three-tier structure with 20pp spread, providing more evidence for the benchmark-conditional story.\n\n- **Separability as confirmatory null.** Reframing infrastructure×methodology separability as a confirmatory result (validating prior isolated-factor studies) rather than a breakthrough is intellectually honest and strengthens the paper's credibility.\n\n- **Fine-tuning contrast.** The inverted variance hierarchy (LR/seed dominate in fine-tuning vs. items dominate in inference) adds conceptual depth and explains why different evaluation modes have different reproducibility conventions.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Weaknesses\n\n### Major\n\n- **Binary data in G-theory.** Applying standard G-theory variance component estimation (Henderson III / REML) to binary (0/1) data is methodologically questionable. The response variable violates normality and homoscedasticity assumptions. Item variance is mechanically linked to item difficulty via the Bernoulli variance function. This affects the headline item_id estimates and all downstream D-study projections. The logprob analysis partially mitigates this, but the paper's primary analysis is on binary data. A generalized G-theory approach (Brennan, 2001, Ch. 12) or a link-function-based decomposition would be more appropriate. At minimum, the paper must discuss this limitation and note that item dominance under binary scoring is partially artifactual.\n\n- **Temperature and ordering as random facets.** With 2-3 temperature levels and 4 systematic ordering rotations, treating these as random facets is questionable. Random-effects estimation requires the observed levels to be exchangeable draws from a larger universe. Temperature levels (0, 0.5, 1.0) are specific design points, not random draws from a temperature distribution. The 4 orderings are systematic rotations, not random permutations of 4!=24 possibilities. Misspecifying fixed effects as random inflates the estimated variance components for these facets and their interactions. The paper should either (a) justify the random-effects assumption for these facets, (b) treat them as fixed and re-estimate, or (c) present both analyses as a sensitivity check.\n\n- **Asymmetric designs across benchmarks weaken cross-benchmark claims.** MMLU uses 3 precisions × 3 temperatures; other benchmarks use 2 × 2. FP32 is absent from non-MMLU experiments. This means precision-related variance on MMLU includes the FP32 degradation effect that is absent from other benchmarks. The claimed gradient in precision effects (MMLU 3.0% → MATH 0.5%) is confounded with the different precision levels tested. The paper should either (a) re-analyze MMLU restricted to BF16/FP16 for the cross-benchmark comparison, or (b) explicitly state the confound.\n\n- **Separability claim is benchmark-conditional but presented as a general finding.** The abstract and contributions list separability as \"<0.5% binary\" but the body reveals GSM8K reaches 3.0% and MATH reaches 2.5%. A finding that holds on 3/5 benchmarks but fails on 2/5 is not a general separability result — it is a benchmark-conditional one. The abstract and conclusions should lead with the conditionality.\n\n### Moderate\n\n- **D-study \"optimal\" prompt/seed counts are not derived.** The paper presents specific prompt and seed counts (3/4 for MMLU, 4/6 for GSM8K) as \"optimal\" without showing the optimization procedure. Are these the minimum counts that don't substantially change the D-study item projection? Are they from a cost-minimization formula? The derivation matters because practitioners will use these numbers directly.\n\n- **Residual component is large and underanalyzed.** Residual ranges from 6% to 18% across benchmarks and analyses. Under model-as-facet analysis, the residual on MMLU drops from 14% to 8%, which is progress, but 8% is still substantial. What is in this residual? Higher-order interactions (3-way and above) are mentioned in passing (\"3-5% total\") but not tabulated. The paper claims higher-order interactions are small, but 3-5% is comparable to many named 2-way interactions (e.g., seed×item at 2.0%). An appendix breaking down the residual would strengthen the paper.\n\n- **HellaSwag clustering with GSM8K is not well-explained.** HellaSwag is a commonsense reasoning / sentence completion task, not a math reasoning task. Its item_id (31%) places it in the same tier as GSM8K (36%), but its infra×method (0.3%) is similar to knowledge benchmarks. The paper groups them in a \"mid\" tier based on item_id alone, but the infra×method pattern suggests HellaSwag behaves differently from GSM8K. The three-tier narrative is cleaner than the data fully support.\n\n- **14B extension is underpowered.** With only 2 models, model-as-facet analysis has essentially 1 df for model effects. The reported model×item = 25% (vs. 21% at 8B) could reflect the specific model pair chosen (dense vs. reasoning-distilled) rather than a scale effect. The paper should note that the 8B→14B comparison confounds scale with architecture diversity.\n\n- **No correction for multiple comparisons.** Many variance components are tested across many benchmarks and metrics. While the bootstrap CIs provide individual component uncertainty, no family-wise error correction is applied. The GSM8K infra×method CI [0.012, 0.048] excludes zero, but is this still significant after correcting for testing the same hypothesis on 5 benchmarks?\n\n### Minor\n\n- **\"Over 3 million\" vs. \"over 4 million.\"** The abstract says \"over 3 million\"; the best-tier paper says \"over 4 million.\" The actual count should be computed and reported exactly.\n\n- **MATH \"other\" = -1.5%.** Negative variance component estimates should be flagged and the handling policy stated (see Section 2.1 above).\n\n- **LayerCast section (8.3) adds little.** The finding that LayerCast provides marginal benefit over BF16 is expected given that precision effects are already small. This could be condensed to 1-2 sentences or moved to an appendix.\n\n- **Table 5 (model-as-facet) shows \"---\" for MATH precision entries.** If precision was not tested on MATH (only BF16/FP16), this should be noted explicitly rather than with a dash.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **How are negative variance component estimates handled?** Henderson Method III can produce negative estimates for near-zero components. Are these set to zero, left negative, or redistributed? This affects the separability claim.\n\n2. **What justifies treating temperature (2-3 levels) as a random rather than fixed facet?** With 3 specific design points, the random-effects assumption is questionable. Have you compared results with temperature as fixed?\n\n3. **For the cross-benchmark comparison, have you re-analyzed MMLU restricted to BF16/FP16 only (excluding FP32)?** This would make the precision facet comparable across benchmarks and test whether the precision gradient survives.\n\n4. **How were the \"optimal\" prompt and seed counts in the D-study determined?** Is there a formal optimization procedure, or were these chosen by inspection?\n\n5. **The bootstrap resamples items but not conditions. Have you considered a double bootstrap (resampling both items and conditions)?** This would capture total uncertainty rather than just item-sampling uncertainty.\n\n6. **How do you address the binary data issue in G-theory estimation?** Standard methods assume continuous responses. Have you considered generalized G-theory or a probit/logit link function approach?\n\n7. **The paper claims \"current practice G<0.25 everywhere\" — what specific \"current practice\" configuration yields this?** Is this 1 prompt × 1 seed × 200 items × 1 temperature × 1 precision? The baseline should be precisely defined.\n\n8. **For GSM8K and MATH, how were answers extracted from generated text?** The extraction method is a potential confound for binary scoring.\n\n9. **Were the 6 prompt templates the same across all 5 benchmarks?** If so, were they designed for MC format specifically, and how appropriate are they for GSM8K/MATH generation tasks?\n\n10. **The fine-tuning experiment uses item-level accuracy across test set items as the response variable, or run-level aggregate accuracy?** If aggregate, G-theory is applied to a single number per run (N=432), which is a very different statistical regime than item-level analysis (N=432×test_items).\n\n---\n\n## 9. Minor Issues\n\n- The G-theory formula in Eq. 2 uses subscript notation that conflates \"conditions\" into a single $c$ — for a 5-facet design, the full formula should be shown or a reference to the exact formula used should be provided.\n- Table 6 (cross-benchmark) reports 384,000 obs for GSM8K/ARC/HellaSwag but the experimental design (4 models × 2 prec × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 2 temps × 4 orderings × 200 items) yields 4×2×6×6×2×4×200 = 230,400. The 384,000 figure is inconsistent — please verify.\n- The paper switches between \"Henderson Method I\" (Section 4.6) and \"Henderson Method III\" (summary). These are different methods — please clarify which was used.\n- Several tables show components that do not sum to 100% without explanation. A footnote about rounding and/or the treatment of negative estimates would help.\n- The Related Work paragraph on training instability cites Dodge et al. (2020) but the bibliography entry has no venue information beyond \"arXiv preprint.\"\n- The Lu et al. (2022) citation appears to be incorrect — the cited paper is about emergent abilities, not option ordering. Verify this reference.\n- The Mesting (2026) bibliography entry says \"ACL\" but the literature map says \"arXiv.\" Verify the venue.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Score and Confidence\n\n### Overall Score: 4/6 (Weak Accept)\n\n### Confidence: High\n\n### Justification\n\nThe paper has substantially improved since Dream #1 by: (a) treating model as a crossed facet, (b) adding bootstrap CIs, (c) expanding to 5 benchmarks with a coherent three-tier narrative, (d) reframing infrastructure separability as confirmatory, (e) expanding the bibliography to 18 references (still below the 25+ target but much improved), and (f) acknowledging item dominance as psychometrically expected while emphasizing the benchmark-conditional gradient as the novel contribution.\n\nThe cross-benchmark variance gradient — the central claim — is well-supported by data across five benchmarks, with honest uncertainty quantification (permutation p=0.003, directional not magnitude claims). The D-study reproducibility budgets fill a genuine practical gap. The metric-conditional finding (G=0.37 vs. G=0.97) is striking and immediately useful.\n\nThe paper falls short of a clear accept (5/6) due to: (a) binary data in standard G-theory without discussion of the methodological limitation, (b) temperature/ordering as random facets with 2-4 levels is dubious, (c) asymmetric designs across benchmarks confound the cross-benchmark comparison of precision effects, and (d) the separability claim is overstated in the abstract given that 2/5 benchmarks show >2% infra×method interaction.\n\nThese issues are addressable in revision:\n- Add a paragraph discussing binary data limitations and cite generalized G-theory\n- Present a sensitivity analysis with temperature as fixed\n- Re-analyze MMLU at BF16/FP16 only for the cross-benchmark precision comparison\n- Qualify the separability claim in the abstract with \"on knowledge benchmarks\"\n\nIf these revisions are made, the paper would be a solid accept (5/6). The experimental scale, honest framing, and actionable D-study budgets make this a useful contribution to the evaluation methodology literature.\n\n### Comparison to Dream #1 Score (4/6 Borderline Reject → 4/6 Weak Accept)\n\nSame numerical score but upgraded from \"borderline reject\" to \"weak accept\" in assessment. The Dream #1 review identified 7 actionable revisions; this version addresses 5 of them (model-as-facet, CIs, bibliography expansion, figures described, reframed contributions). The remaining 2 (separability threshold derivation, item dominance tautology acknowledgment) are partially addressed. The net effect is that the paper moves from \"below acceptance threshold\" to \"at the acceptance threshold, contingent on minor revisions.\" The methodological concerns raised in this review (binary data, temperature-as-random, asymmetric designs) are new issues not flagged in Dream #1 but are moderate rather than fatal.\n","project":"nlp_variance_gtheory","tier":"median","dream_id":"dream_20260520T222113","overall_score":"weak accept"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":732,"cache_read_input_tokens":90192,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":732,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01RdahfyKVtwMwAfTJrn7pUJ","session_id":"e13969ce-5d2d-4a82-903b-b87fae4e296f","uuid":"09404e11-da04-44f0-ab9b-0e4afab83526"}
