{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XyqRPEpqfbg8bPCwzkLMYF","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260520T222113\n3\ttier: best\n4\toverall_score: weak accept\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-20T23:35:45.464213+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t---\n9\tdream_id: dream_20260520T222113\n10\ttier: best\n11\treviewer: R2\n12\tfocus: Novelty, Related Work Positioning, Significance/Impact\n13\ttimestamp: 2026-05-20\n14\t---\n15\t\n16\t# Reviewer R2: Novelty, Related Work, and Significance\n17\t\n18\t## 1. Summary\n19\t\n20\tThis paper applies Generalizability Theory (G-theory) to LLM evaluation in a multi-benchmark setting, crossing infrastructure facets (numerical precision, GPU hardware) with methodology facets (prompt template, random seed, temperature, option ordering) and a measurement facet (item selection) across five benchmarks (MMLU, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag, GSM8K, MATH), six models (7--14B), and 4M+ evaluation records. Three claims are advanced: (1) a cross-benchmark variance gradient where item_id share drops monotonically from 56% (MMLU) to 21% (MATH), with CoT stochasticity shifting variance into temperature x item and seed x item interactions; (2) a confirmatory separability null -- direct infrastructure x methodology interactions < 0.3%; (3) D-study protocol reversals showing MMLU is item-limited (420 items for G >= 0.90) while GSM8K is seed-limited (10 seeds), with current practice yielding G < 0.25 everywhere. Extensions include 14B scale validation, a fine-tuning G-study with RoBERTa-large, and LayerCast precision mitigation.\n21\t\n22\t## 2. Novelty Assessment\n23\t\n24\t### Claim 1 (Cross-benchmark variance gradient): Moderate-to-high novelty.\n25\t\n26\tThis is the paper's strongest novel contribution. No prior work has demonstrated a systematic, monotonic shift in variance structure across multiple benchmarks positioned along a reasoning-intensity continuum. The 35pp gradient from MMLU to MATH, with the mechanistic explanation via CoT stochasticity, is a genuine empirical finding that unifies apparently contradictory conclusions from prior single-benchmark studies (Sclar on prompt sensitivity, Pezeshkpour on ordering, Yuan on precision). The \"unifying explanation\" framing is well-executed: these studies are not contradictory -- they studied different benchmarks at different positions on the gradient.\n27\t\n28\tHowever, the finding that item_id dominates is expected from measurement theory (Brennan, 2001, Ch. 3). Any assessment instrument with wide difficulty range will show item variance as the largest source. The paper now acknowledges this appropriately (unlike the earlier draft), but the contribution is in the quantification and the gradient, not in item dominance per se.\n29\t\n30\t### Claim 2 (Confirmatory separability): Low novelty, moderate utility.\n31\t\n32\tThis is a confirmatory null result. Infrastructure x methodology interactions are < 0.3% -- meaning that the infrastructure crossing, which the paper positions as a key methodological advance over Mesting (2026), yields almost nothing. The paper is now honest about this (framing it as \"confirmatory\" rather than \"discovery\"), which is the right move. The nuanced decomposition into direct vs. item-conditional interactions (Table 6) adds value: aggregate separability holds, but both factor types interact with items. This is useful methodological validation, but it does not constitute a primary novelty claim.\n33\t\n34\tThe practical implication is clear: researchers running isolated-factor studies (e.g., studying only prompt sensitivity) can trust their aggregate conclusions, but should be cautious about item-level generalizations. This is valuable guidance even if not surprising.\n35\t\n36\t### Claim 3 (D-study protocol reversal): Moderate novelty, high practical value.\n37\t\n38\tThe finding that MMLU needs 420 items while GSM8K needs 10 seeds -- and that the facet effectiveness ratio inverts from 14.2:1 (MMLU) to 0.8:1 (GSM8K) -- is actionable and non-obvious. The \"no universal recipe\" conclusion directly challenges the standardized evaluation protocols being adopted by leaderboards. The D-study projection grids (Tables 9-10) are the most practically useful output of the paper.\n39\t\n40\tThe current-practice G < 0.25 finding is alarming and newsworthy, though its impact depends on whether the community adopts G-theory as a reliability metric. The comparison to educational measurement's G >= 0.80 threshold is effective rhetoric.\n41\t\n42\t### Collective novelty: The three claims together are stronger than any individual claim.\n43\t\n44\tThe cross-benchmark gradient (Claim 1) explains the protocol reversal (Claim 3), and the separability null (Claim 2) validates the independent study of each factor type. The narrative coherence is good. The paper is best understood as a large-scale empirical study that applies a well-established framework (G-theory) to a new domain (multi-benchmark LLM evaluation) and discovers a systematic empirical regularity (the gradient). This is a meaningful contribution, though not a methodological breakthrough.\n45\t\n46\t### Incrementality over Mesting (2026).\n47\t\n48\tThe paper clearly advances over Mesting in three dimensions: (a) five benchmarks vs. one, (b) infrastructure crossing, (c) model as crossed facet. The infrastructure crossing yields near-null results, so (b) is a confirmatory contribution. The multi-benchmark gradient and model-as-facet analysis are the genuine advances. The paper's four stated differences from Mesting (Section 2, paragraph 3) are accurate and honest.\n49\t\n50\tOne important difference the paper does not sufficiently emphasize: Mesting found judge identity as the dominant variance source (43.8%), while this paper fixes the judge and finds item_id dominant. These are complementary findings -- Mesting's design includes judge variance that would dominate everything else; this paper's design excludes judge to reveal the item/methodology/infrastructure structure underneath. This complementarity should be discussed more explicitly.\n51\t\n52\t## 3. Related Work Completeness\n53\t\n54\tThe bibliography has improved dramatically from the earlier 7-reference draft to 22 references. The four Related Work paragraphs are well-organized and the positioning against prior work is largely fair and accurate.\n55\t\n56\t### Adequately covered:\n57\t- Variance sources in LLM evaluation (Sclar, Pezeshkpour, Lu, Yuan, Biderman) -- good coverage\n58\t- ANOVA-based decomposition (Bouthillier, Henderson, Dodge, Sellam, Dehghani) -- well-positioned\n59\t- G-theory in NLP (Mesting, Brennan, Shavelson) -- properly cited with explicit differentiation\n60\t- IRT in NLP (Lalor, Rodriguez, Polo, Perlitz) -- good, with IRT/G-theory complementarity noted\n61\t\n62\t### Missing or under-discussed references:\n63\t\n64\t1. **Nalisnick et al. (2024), GeneralizIT** -- a G-theory software package for ML. Directly relevant to the question of whether this paper's analytical framework is novel or already available as a tool. Should be cited in the G-theory paragraph.\n65\t\n66\t2. **Kunievsky et al. (2025), prompt sensitivity decomposition** -- decomposes prompt variance into intent/articulation/model uncertainty. Relevant to interpreting the prompt x item interaction component. The paper's related_work_notes mention it but the paper does not cite it.\n67\t\n68\t3. **Brittlebench (2026)** -- prompt brittleness benchmark, ~50% prompt variance. In the project's own literature map but absent from the paper. Directly relevant to the prompt facet findings.\n69\t\n70\t4. **Bui et al. (2025), seed sensitivity in fine-tuning** -- RoBERTa/Llama seed sensitivity study. Directly relevant to Section 8.2 (fine-tuning G-study). In the literature map but not cited.\n71\t\n72\t5. **ThinkingMachines (2025), batch invariance failure** -- 80/1000 unique completions at T=0 from batch-size dependence. The paper uses default vLLM settings without batch-invariant kernels; this reference would explain why seed x item effects are non-zero even at T=0. Not cited.\n73\t\n74\t6. **Vania et al. (2021)** -- IRT item properties in NLP. Listed in the bibliography but not discussed in the Related Work text. Should be integrated into the IRT paragraph.\n75\t\n76\t7. **Mizrahi et al. (2024)** -- cited in the introduction but not in Related Work. Multi-prompt evaluation advocacy. Should appear in the variance sources paragraph.\n77\t\n78\t### Positioning fairness:\n79\t\n80\tThe paper's positioning against prior work is generally fair. The claim that \"each prior study isolates at most two variance sources\" is accurate for the cited studies. The differentiation from Mesting is honest and specific. The IRT/G-theory complementarity framing is appropriate and avoids dismissing IRT work.\n81\t\n82\tOne concern: the paper states that prior ANOVA-based studies \"lack the D-study machinery for projecting reliability under alternative designs.\" This is technically true but slightly misleading -- bootstrap-based approaches can achieve similar projections without G-theory. The advantage of D-study is its closed-form projections, not that the question is unanswerable otherwise.\n83\t\n84\t## 4. Significance and Impact\n85\t\n86\t### Will this paper change how people evaluate LLMs?\n87\t\n88\tPartially. The D-study budgets are immediately actionable for benchmark designers and evaluation framework maintainers (e.g., lm-evaluation-harness, HELM). The \"G < 0.25 everywhere\" finding is a compelling indictment of current practice. However, adoption depends on:\n89\t\n90\t1. Whether the community accepts G-theory as the right reliability framework (vs. IRT, bootstrap, or simple confidence intervals)\n91\t2. Whether the computational cost of multi-seed, multi-prompt evaluation is acceptable\n92\t3. Whether leaderboard operators are incentivized to change practices\n93\t\n94\tThe paper would benefit from a concrete comparison: \"Running MMLU with the recommended budget (420 items, 3 prompts, 2 seeds = 2,520 evaluations) costs X GPU-hours per model, compared to Y GPU-hours for current practice.\" Without cost estimates, the budgets are theoretical.\n95\t\n96\t### Are the D-study budgets practically useful?\n97\t\n98\tYes, with caveats. The specific numbers (420 items for MMLU, 10 seeds for GSM8K) are directly useful. The facet effectiveness ratios (Table 12) provide a decision framework. The projection grids (Tables 9-10) allow practitioners to find their own cost-reliability tradeoff.\n99\t\n100\tThe main limitation is that the budgets are derived from 7-14B open-weight models. API-based models (GPT-4, Claude) may have different variance structures due to opaque infrastructure, quantization, and routing. The paper acknowledges this but does not quantify the risk.\n101\t\n102\t### Is \"no universal recipe\" surprising or obvious?\n103\t\n104\tModerately surprising in its specificity. The qualitative intuition that \"different benchmarks might need different protocols\" is not new. But the quantitative demonstration that the facet effectiveness ratio inverts from 14.2:1 to 0.8:1 across benchmarks, and that the same practitioner should use 420 items + 2 seeds for MMLU but 150 items + 10 seeds for GSM8K, is non-obvious and actionable. The counter-intuitive finding that knowledge benchmarks yield lower G than reasoning benchmarks (MMLU 0.36 vs. GSM8K 0.55 under full factorial) is genuinely surprising and well-explained.\n105\t\n106\t## 5. Framing and Narrative\n107\t\n108\t### Strengths:\n109\t- The three-claim structure is clean and well-supported.\n110\t- The \"unifying explanation\" framing for the gradient is effective.\n111\t- The Discussion section is balanced, with a thorough Limitations paragraph.\n112\t- The engine confound discovery is presented with appropriate before/after validation.\n113\t- The paper correctly frames infrastructure crossing as confirmatory rather than primary novelty.\n114\t\n115\t### Weaknesses:\n116\t- **Zero figures.** This is a serious deficiency for a variance decomposition paper. The distilled writing patterns from this field identify stacked-bar charts as an expected idiom (Mesting, Brittlebench). The cross-benchmark gradient (Table 5) would be far more compelling as a stacked-bar figure. The D-study projections (Tables 9-10) cry out for line plots of G vs. facet allocation. The facet effectiveness inversion (Table 12) would be intuitive as a crossing-lines plot. With 14 tables and 0 figures, the paper is dense and visually monotonous. This is a competitive disadvantage at a top venue.\n117\t\n118\t- **The paper title promises \"Evaluation Metrics\" but delivers only binary scoring.** The abstract and main text focus entirely on binary correct scoring. Logprob-based scoring is relegated to a one-paragraph appendix (Appendix B). The paper framing documents mention \"metric-conditional budgets\" as a core claim, but the submitted paper has largely abandoned this dimension. Either deliver the metric analysis or remove \"Evaluation Metrics\" from the title.\n119\t\n120\t- **The fine-tuning section (8.2) feels bolted on.** RoBERTa-large fine-tuning on SST-2/MNLI/RTE/COPA is architecturally distant from the 7-14B decoder inference setting. The variance hierarchy comparison (Table 15) is interesting but the cross-paradigm generalization is weak. This section would be stronger as a standalone follow-up study or should be explicitly qualified as \"preliminary evidence.\"\n121\t\n122\t## 6. Scope Concerns\n123\t\n124\t### Is 5 benchmarks x 6 models x 7-14B sufficient?\n125\t\n126\tFor the cross-benchmark gradient: yes, 5 benchmarks spanning knowledge-to-reasoning is adequate. The monotonic gradient with non-overlapping bootstrap CIs (Table 8) provides statistical support. Adding more benchmarks (e.g., GPQA, TruthfulQA, HumanEval) would strengthen the gradient but is not strictly necessary.\n127\t\n128\tFor model generality: the 4 x 7-9B models show consistent patterns (per-model tables confirm the gradient within each model), and the 14B extension (Section 8.1) falls within the 8B range. However:\n129\t\n130\t1. **No 70B+ models.** Larger models may have qualitatively different variance structures (e.g., higher accuracy compresses item_id, stronger reasoning reduces CoT stochasticity). The paper cannot claim generality beyond the 7-14B scale.\n131\t\n132\t2. **No proprietary API models.** GPT-4, Claude, Gemini have opaque infrastructure (unknown precision, routing, batching). The separability null may not hold when infrastructure is uncontrolled. Mesting (2026) studied API models; this paper studies open-weight models. The complementarity is noted but not deeply explored.\n133\t\n134\t3. **No non-English benchmarks.** All five benchmarks are English-only. Cross-lingual variance structure could differ systematically (e.g., tokenization effects on item difficulty).\n135\t\n136\t4. **No generation-based benchmarks.** All benchmarks are multiple-choice or short-answer. Open-ended generation tasks (summarization, translation, creative writing) may have fundamentally different variance structures where temperature and seed effects dominate even more.\n137\t\n138\t5. **Only 200 items per benchmark.** MMLU has ~14K items, GSM8K has ~8K. The 200-item stratified sample captures the difficulty distribution but may miss tail effects. The resampling stability test (Section 4.3) partially addresses this but only for MMLU.\n139\t\n140\t## 7. Strengths (bulleted)\n141\t\n142\t- **Cross-benchmark variance gradient is a genuine, well-supported empirical contribution** that unifies contradictory findings from prior single-benchmark studies. The monotonic 35pp gradient from MMLU to MATH, with mechanistic explanation via CoT stochasticity, is the paper's signature finding.\n143\t\n144\t- **Massive experimental scale**: 4M+ evaluation records, 5 benchmarks, 6 models, crossed factorial designs. Few empirical papers in this area match the breadth.\n145\t\n146\t- **D-study reproducibility budgets are immediately actionable**: specific item/prompt/seed allocations for G >= 0.90, benchmark-specific, with projection grids for cost-reliability tradeoffs.\n147\t\n148\t- **Engine confound discovery and correction** (vLLM V0 vs. V1 routing for different precisions) is a standalone methodological contribution valuable to any researcher comparing precision modes.\n149\t\n150\t- **Honest framing of the separability null**: infrastructure crossing framed as confirmatory rather than primary novelty, with nuanced decomposition into aggregate vs. item-conditional interactions.\n151\t\n152\t- **Comprehensive robustness checks**: item resampling stability, REML vs. Henderson III comparison, bootstrap CIs, per-model breakdowns, 14B scale validation, cross-estimation-method concordance.\n153\t\n154\t- **Bibliography substantially improved** over earlier drafts: 22 references covering variance sources, ANOVA decomposition, G-theory, and IRT literature.\n155\t\n156\t- **Counter-intuitive G pattern** (knowledge benchmarks yield lower G than reasoning benchmarks) is well-explained and adds insight beyond the headline gradient finding.\n157\t\n158\t## 8. Weaknesses (bulleted)\n159\t\n160\t- **Zero figures in a variance decomposition paper** is a serious presentation deficiency. Stacked-bar charts, D-study curves, and gradient visualizations are expected field idioms (Mesting 2026, Brittlebench 2026). 14 tables + 0 figures makes the paper dense and hard to scan.\n161\t\n162\t- **Infrastructure crossing is the claimed methodological advance over Mesting but yields near-null results** (< 0.3% direct interactions). The main findings (gradient, D-study budgets) would emerge from a Mesting-style design with fixed infrastructure. The paper is honest about this but it weakens the novelty argument.\n163\t\n164\t- **Title promises \"Evaluation Metrics\" but the paper delivers only binary scoring** in the main text. Logprob analysis is a one-paragraph appendix. The metric-conditional dimension from the paper framing is largely absent.\n165\t\n166\t- **No models above 14B.** Larger models may compress item_id and reduce CoT stochasticity, potentially altering the gradient. Generality claims should be scoped to 7-14B.\n167\t\n168\t- **Fine-tuning extension (RoBERTa-large, encoder-only, classification)** is architecturally distant from the decoder inference setting. Cross-paradigm generalization is weak; the section feels appended rather than integrated.\n169\t\n170\t- **Missing references**: GeneralizIT (2024), Kunievsky et al. (2025), Brittlebench (2026), Bui et al. (2025), ThinkingMachines (2025) -- all in the project's own literature map but absent from the paper. These are relevant to the G-theory tooling, prompt decomposition, and fine-tuning variance discussions.\n171\t\n172\t- **No cost analysis for recommended budgets.** The D-study projections recommend specific item/prompt/seed allocations but do not estimate the GPU-hour cost of implementing these recommendations. Without cost, the budgets are theoretical.\n173\t\n174\t- **200-item samples from much larger benchmarks.** Resampling stability is tested only for MMLU. The other four benchmarks lack resampling validation, leaving open the possibility that the gradient is partially driven by item sampling artifacts on specific benchmarks.\n175\t\n176\t## 9. Questions for Authors\n177\t\n178\t1. The cross-benchmark gradient is your strongest finding. Could you demonstrate that it holds under logprob-based scoring as well? The one-paragraph Appendix B suggests item_id drops to 42.8% on MMLU, but does the full gradient (MMLU > ARC > HellaSwag > GSM8K > MATH) hold under logprob scoring? If not, is the gradient an artifact of binary scoring thresholds?\n179\t\n180\t2. Your D-study recommends 420 items + 3 prompts + 2 seeds for MMLU (2,520 total evaluations). How does this compare to Polo et al.'s tinyBenchmarks recommendation for MMLU? If IRT-based subsampling achieves reliable rankings with fewer items, what does G-theory add?\n181\t\n182\t3. DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B shows elevated temperature x item (8.8%) and seed x item (5.2%) on MMLU -- above the 8B range (Table 13). You attribute this to \"reasoning distillation introducing implicit chain-of-thought tendencies.\" Can you verify this mechanistically? Does DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B produce longer or more variable outputs on MMLU items compared to the other models?\n183\t\n184\t4. The separability null holds for 2 GPU types (Section 6). How confident are you that this extends to, say, A100 vs. H100 vs. B200 comparisons where the architectural differences (TF32 tensor cores, FP8 support, different memory bandwidth) are more substantial than between two GPUs of similar generation?\n185\t\n186\t5. You fix the judge model, unlike Mesting who includes judge as a facet. Since Mesting found judge identity as the dominant source (43.8%), your entire variance structure sits \"underneath\" the judge variance. How should practitioners interpret your budgets if they use LLM-as-judge evaluation (increasingly common)? Would your gradient hold if judge were included as a facet, or would judge variance dominate and flatten the cross-benchmark differences?\n187\t\n188\t6. The fine-tuning G-study finds item_id at rank-1 across all tasks (35.8-43.8%). But Bui et al. (2025) report extreme seed variance (RTES variance = 18.22) for RoBERTa on RTE. How do your RTE fine-tuning results compare to Bui et al.? Are the magnitudes consistent?\n189\t\n190\t7. Current-practice G < 0.25 is your most alarming finding. Have you computed what fraction of published leaderboard rankings would change if evaluations were repeated with different seeds/prompts? A \"rank instability\" analysis (e.g., what percentage of model-pair orderings flip under re-evaluation) would make the inadequacy finding more concrete than an abstract G coefficient.\n191\t\n192\t8. The paper uses Henderson's Method III with REML as sensitivity check and reports they agree within 1.4pp. Have you considered Bayesian variance component estimation (e.g., via Stan or brms)? This would give full posterior distributions on variance components rather than point estimates + bootstrap CIs, and would handle the near-zero infrastructure components more gracefully.\n193\t\n194\t## 10. Minor Issues\n195\t\n196\t- Table 5 header says \"4 models pooled\" but the design description mentions \"4 models x 2 precisions x ...\" -- clarify whether model is pooled or crossed in this analysis.\n197\t- Table 7 (5-benchmark gradient) shows G_full for GSM8K (0.55) higher than MATH (0.50), breaking the monotonic pattern. The text does not explain this reversal; add a brief note.\n198\t- The abstract says \"over 4 million evaluation records\" but summing the reported record counts (1,036,800 + 1,382,400 + 460,800 + 691,200 + 345,600 + 172,800 + fine-tuning) exceeds 4M. Provide an exact total or say \"approximately.\"\n199\t- Section 3.2 says \"2-3 levels\" for temperature but does not specify which benchmarks use 2 vs. 3 levels. Clarify.\n200\t- Table 1 (MMLU variance): the \"infra x method\" row aggregates multiple interaction terms. Break this out or provide the individual terms in an appendix.\n201\t- The paper references \"Section 7\" (comprehensive analysis) as Section \\ref{sec:comprehensive} but the numbering in the LaTeX may not match if figures are added later. Verify cross-references.\n202\t- Appendix C on higher-order interactions is interesting (temp x seed x item = 1.8%) but could be expanded. Does this three-way interaction show a benchmark gradient similar to the two-way interactions?\n203\t\n204\t## 11. Score\n205\t\n206\t**Score: 4/6 (Weak Accept)**\n207\t\n208\tThis is a well-executed, large-scale empirical study that applies G-theory to multi-benchmark LLM evaluation and discovers a genuine empirical regularity (the cross-benchmark variance gradient). The D-study budgets are practically useful, the experimental scale is impressive, and the paper has improved substantially from earlier drafts -- the bibliography is now adequate, the framing is honest about confirmatory vs. novel contributions, and the robustness checks are thorough. The cross-benchmark gradient as a unifying explanation for contradictory prior findings is the paper's strongest contribution and clears the novelty bar for a top venue.\n209\t\n210\tThe paper is held back by: (a) zero figures, which is a serious presentation weakness for a variance decomposition paper; (b) the infrastructure crossing being the claimed advance over Mesting yet yielding near-null results; (c) missing metric-conditional analysis despite the title; and (d) scope limitations (7-14B only, no API models, encoder-only fine-tuning). The first issue (figures) is easily fixable and would likely move the paper to a solid accept. The others are structural but not disqualifying.\n211\t\n212\tThe balance tips toward accept because the cross-benchmark gradient is genuinely novel, the D-study budgets fill a real practical gap, and the \"G < 0.25 everywhere\" finding is an important wake-up call for the field. The paper would benefit from another revision addressing the figure deficit and the metric-conditional analysis, but the core contribution is sound.\n213\t\n214\t## 12. Confidence\n215\t\n216\t**Confidence: High**\n217\t\n218\tI am familiar with G-theory (Brennan 2001, Shavelson & Webb 1991), the LLM evaluation reproducibility literature (Sclar, Pezeshkpour, Yuan, Biderman, Mesting), and the IRT/benchmark efficiency literature (Polo, Rodriguez, Perlitz). I have reviewed the full paper text, the related work notes, and the paper framing documents. My main uncertainty is whether models above 14B would substantially alter the gradient pattern -- this is an empirical question the paper cannot answer with its current design.\n219\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e13969ce-5d2d-4a82-903b-b87fae4e296f","uuid":"e1bf12d9-b2e7-4638-b0e8-6c846eca4fd5","timestamp":"2026-05-20T23:36:31.364Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519/projects/nlp_variance_gtheory/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T222113/best/review_report.md","content":"---\ndream_id: dream_20260520T222113\ntier: best\noverall_score: weak accept\ntimestamp: 2026-05-20T23:35:45.464213+00:00\n---\n\n---\ndream_id: dream_20260520T222113\ntier: best\nreviewer: R2\nfocus: Novelty, Related Work Positioning, Significance/Impact\ntimestamp: 2026-05-20\n---\n\n# Reviewer R2: Novelty, Related Work, and Significance\n\n## 1. Summary\n\nThis paper applies Generalizability Theory (G-theory) to LLM evaluation in a multi-benchmark setting, crossing infrastructure facets (numerical precision, GPU hardware) with methodology facets (prompt template, random seed, temperature, option ordering) and a measurement facet (item selection) across five benchmarks (MMLU, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag, GSM8K, MATH), six models (7--14B), and 4M+ evaluation records. Three claims are advanced: (1) a cross-benchmark variance gradient where item_id share drops monotonically from 56% (MMLU) to 21% (MATH), with CoT stochasticity shifting variance into temperature x item and seed x item interactions; (2) a confirmatory separability null -- direct infrastructure x methodology interactions < 0.3%; (3) D-study protocol reversals showing MMLU is item-limited (420 items for G >= 0.90) while GSM8K is seed-limited (10 seeds), with current practice yielding G < 0.25 everywhere. Extensions include 14B scale validation, a fine-tuning G-study with RoBERTa-large, and LayerCast precision mitigation.\n\n## 2. Novelty Assessment\n\n### Claim 1 (Cross-benchmark variance gradient): Moderate-to-high novelty.\n\nThis is the paper's strongest novel contribution. No prior work has demonstrated a systematic, monotonic shift in variance structure across multiple benchmarks positioned along a reasoning-intensity continuum. The 35pp gradient from MMLU to MATH, with the mechanistic explanation via CoT stochasticity, is a genuine empirical finding that unifies apparently contradictory conclusions from prior single-benchmark studies (Sclar on prompt sensitivity, Pezeshkpour on ordering, Yuan on precision). The \"unifying explanation\" framing is well-executed: these studies are not contradictory -- they studied different benchmarks at different positions on the gradient.\n\nHowever, the finding that item_id dominates is expected from measurement theory (Brennan, 2001, Ch. 3). Any assessment instrument with wide difficulty range will show item variance as the largest source. The paper now acknowledges this appropriately (unlike the earlier draft), but the contribution is in the quantification and the gradient, not in item dominance per se.\n\n### Claim 2 (Confirmatory separability): Low novelty, moderate utility.\n\nThis is a confirmatory null result. Infrastructure x methodology interactions are < 0.3% -- meaning that the infrastructure crossing, which the paper positions as a key methodological advance over Mesting (2026), yields almost nothing. The paper is now honest about this (framing it as \"confirmatory\" rather than \"discovery\"), which is the right move. The nuanced decomposition into direct vs. item-conditional interactions (Table 6) adds value: aggregate separability holds, but both factor types interact with items. This is useful methodological validation, but it does not constitute a primary novelty claim.\n\nThe practical implication is clear: researchers running isolated-factor studies (e.g., studying only prompt sensitivity) can trust their aggregate conclusions, but should be cautious about item-level generalizations. This is valuable guidance even if not surprising.\n\n### Claim 3 (D-study protocol reversal): Moderate novelty, high practical value.\n\nThe finding that MMLU needs 420 items while GSM8K needs 10 seeds -- and that the facet effectiveness ratio inverts from 14.2:1 (MMLU) to 0.8:1 (GSM8K) -- is actionable and non-obvious. The \"no universal recipe\" conclusion directly challenges the standardized evaluation protocols being adopted by leaderboards. The D-study projection grids (Tables 9-10) are the most practically useful output of the paper.\n\nThe current-practice G < 0.25 finding is alarming and newsworthy, though its impact depends on whether the community adopts G-theory as a reliability metric. The comparison to educational measurement's G >= 0.80 threshold is effective rhetoric.\n\n### Collective novelty: The three claims together are stronger than any individual claim.\n\nThe cross-benchmark gradient (Claim 1) explains the protocol reversal (Claim 3), and the separability null (Claim 2) validates the independent study of each factor type. The narrative coherence is good. The paper is best understood as a large-scale empirical study that applies a well-established framework (G-theory) to a new domain (multi-benchmark LLM evaluation) and discovers a systematic empirical regularity (the gradient). This is a meaningful contribution, though not a methodological breakthrough.\n\n### Incrementality over Mesting (2026).\n\nThe paper clearly advances over Mesting in three dimensions: (a) five benchmarks vs. one, (b) infrastructure crossing, (c) model as crossed facet. The infrastructure crossing yields near-null results, so (b) is a confirmatory contribution. The multi-benchmark gradient and model-as-facet analysis are the genuine advances. The paper's four stated differences from Mesting (Section 2, paragraph 3) are accurate and honest.\n\nOne important difference the paper does not sufficiently emphasize: Mesting found judge identity as the dominant variance source (43.8%), while this paper fixes the judge and finds item_id dominant. These are complementary findings -- Mesting's design includes judge variance that would dominate everything else; this paper's design excludes judge to reveal the item/methodology/infrastructure structure underneath. This complementarity should be discussed more explicitly.\n\n## 3. Related Work Completeness\n\nThe bibliography has improved dramatically from the earlier 7-reference draft to 22 references. The four Related Work paragraphs are well-organized and the positioning against prior work is largely fair and accurate.\n\n### Adequately covered:\n- Variance sources in LLM evaluation (Sclar, Pezeshkpour, Lu, Yuan, Biderman) -- good coverage\n- ANOVA-based decomposition (Bouthillier, Henderson, Dodge, Sellam, Dehghani) -- well-positioned\n- G-theory in NLP (Mesting, Brennan, Shavelson) -- properly cited with explicit differentiation\n- IRT in NLP (Lalor, Rodriguez, Polo, Perlitz) -- good, with IRT/G-theory complementarity noted\n\n### Missing or under-discussed references:\n\n1. **Nalisnick et al. (2024), GeneralizIT** -- a G-theory software package for ML. Directly relevant to the question of whether this paper's analytical framework is novel or already available as a tool. Should be cited in the G-theory paragraph.\n\n2. **Kunievsky et al. (2025), prompt sensitivity decomposition** -- decomposes prompt variance into intent/articulation/model uncertainty. Relevant to interpreting the prompt x item interaction component. The paper's related_work_notes mention it but the paper does not cite it.\n\n3. **Brittlebench (2026)** -- prompt brittleness benchmark, ~50% prompt variance. In the project's own literature map but absent from the paper. Directly relevant to the prompt facet findings.\n\n4. **Bui et al. (2025), seed sensitivity in fine-tuning** -- RoBERTa/Llama seed sensitivity study. Directly relevant to Section 8.2 (fine-tuning G-study). In the literature map but not cited.\n\n5. **ThinkingMachines (2025), batch invariance failure** -- 80/1000 unique completions at T=0 from batch-size dependence. The paper uses default vLLM settings without batch-invariant kernels; this reference would explain why seed x item effects are non-zero even at T=0. Not cited.\n\n6. **Vania et al. (2021)** -- IRT item properties in NLP. Listed in the bibliography but not discussed in the Related Work text. Should be integrated into the IRT paragraph.\n\n7. **Mizrahi et al. (2024)** -- cited in the introduction but not in Related Work. Multi-prompt evaluation advocacy. Should appear in the variance sources paragraph.\n\n### Positioning fairness:\n\nThe paper's positioning against prior work is generally fair. The claim that \"each prior study isolates at most two variance sources\" is accurate for the cited studies. The differentiation from Mesting is honest and specific. The IRT/G-theory complementarity framing is appropriate and avoids dismissing IRT work.\n\nOne concern: the paper states that prior ANOVA-based studies \"lack the D-study machinery for projecting reliability under alternative designs.\" This is technically true but slightly misleading -- bootstrap-based approaches can achieve similar projections without G-theory. The advantage of D-study is its closed-form projections, not that the question is unanswerable otherwise.\n\n## 4. Significance and Impact\n\n### Will this paper change how people evaluate LLMs?\n\nPartially. The D-study budgets are immediately actionable for benchmark designers and evaluation framework maintainers (e.g., lm-evaluation-harness, HELM). The \"G < 0.25 everywhere\" finding is a compelling indictment of current practice. However, adoption depends on:\n\n1. Whether the community accepts G-theory as the right reliability framework (vs. IRT, bootstrap, or simple confidence intervals)\n2. Whether the computational cost of multi-seed, multi-prompt evaluation is acceptable\n3. Whether leaderboard operators are incentivized to change practices\n\nThe paper would benefit from a concrete comparison: \"Running MMLU with the recommended budget (420 items, 3 prompts, 2 seeds = 2,520 evaluations) costs X GPU-hours per model, compared to Y GPU-hours for current practice.\" Without cost estimates, the budgets are theoretical.\n\n### Are the D-study budgets practically useful?\n\nYes, with caveats. The specific numbers (420 items for MMLU, 10 seeds for GSM8K) are directly useful. The facet effectiveness ratios (Table 12) provide a decision framework. The projection grids (Tables 9-10) allow practitioners to find their own cost-reliability tradeoff.\n\nThe main limitation is that the budgets are derived from 7-14B open-weight models. API-based models (GPT-4, Claude) may have different variance structures due to opaque infrastructure, quantization, and routing. The paper acknowledges this but does not quantify the risk.\n\n### Is \"no universal recipe\" surprising or obvious?\n\nModerately surprising in its specificity. The qualitative intuition that \"different benchmarks might need different protocols\" is not new. But the quantitative demonstration that the facet effectiveness ratio inverts from 14.2:1 to 0.8:1 across benchmarks, and that the same practitioner should use 420 items + 2 seeds for MMLU but 150 items + 10 seeds for GSM8K, is non-obvious and actionable. The counter-intuitive finding that knowledge benchmarks yield lower G than reasoning benchmarks (MMLU 0.36 vs. GSM8K 0.55 under full factorial) is genuinely surprising and well-explained.\n\n## 5. Framing and Narrative\n\n### Strengths:\n- The three-claim structure is clean and well-supported.\n- The \"unifying explanation\" framing for the gradient is effective.\n- The Discussion section is balanced, with a thorough Limitations paragraph.\n- The engine confound discovery is presented with appropriate before/after validation.\n- The paper correctly frames infrastructure crossing as confirmatory rather than primary novelty.\n\n### Weaknesses:\n- **Zero figures.** This is a serious deficiency for a variance decomposition paper. The distilled writing patterns from this field identify stacked-bar charts as an expected idiom (Mesting, Brittlebench). The cross-benchmark gradient (Table 5) would be far more compelling as a stacked-bar figure. The D-study projections (Tables 9-10) cry out for line plots of G vs. facet allocation. The facet effectiveness inversion (Table 12) would be intuitive as a crossing-lines plot. With 14 tables and 0 figures, the paper is dense and visually monotonous. This is a competitive disadvantage at a top venue.\n\n- **The paper title promises \"Evaluation Metrics\" but delivers only binary scoring.** The abstract and main text focus entirely on binary correct scoring. Logprob-based scoring is relegated to a one-paragraph appendix (Appendix B). The paper framing documents mention \"metric-conditional budgets\" as a core claim, but the submitted paper has largely abandoned this dimension. Either deliver the metric analysis or remove \"Evaluation Metrics\" from the title.\n\n- **The fine-tuning section (8.2) feels bolted on.** RoBERTa-large fine-tuning on SST-2/MNLI/RTE/COPA is architecturally distant from the 7-14B decoder inference setting. The variance hierarchy comparison (Table 15) is interesting but the cross-paradigm generalization is weak. This section would be stronger as a standalone follow-up study or should be explicitly qualified as \"preliminary evidence.\"\n\n## 6. Scope Concerns\n\n### Is 5 benchmarks x 6 models x 7-14B sufficient?\n\nFor the cross-benchmark gradient: yes, 5 benchmarks spanning knowledge-to-reasoning is adequate. The monotonic gradient with non-overlapping bootstrap CIs (Table 8) provides statistical support. Adding more benchmarks (e.g., GPQA, TruthfulQA, HumanEval) would strengthen the gradient but is not strictly necessary.\n\nFor model generality: the 4 x 7-9B models show consistent patterns (per-model tables confirm the gradient within each model), and the 14B extension (Section 8.1) falls within the 8B range. However:\n\n1. **No 70B+ models.** Larger models may have qualitatively different variance structures (e.g., higher accuracy compresses item_id, stronger reasoning reduces CoT stochasticity). The paper cannot claim generality beyond the 7-14B scale.\n\n2. **No proprietary API models.** GPT-4, Claude, Gemini have opaque infrastructure (unknown precision, routing, batching). The separability null may not hold when infrastructure is uncontrolled. Mesting (2026) studied API models; this paper studies open-weight models. The complementarity is noted but not deeply explored.\n\n3. **No non-English benchmarks.** All five benchmarks are English-only. Cross-lingual variance structure could differ systematically (e.g., tokenization effects on item difficulty).\n\n4. **No generation-based benchmarks.** All benchmarks are multiple-choice or short-answer. Open-ended generation tasks (summarization, translation, creative writing) may have fundamentally different variance structures where temperature and seed effects dominate even more.\n\n5. **Only 200 items per benchmark.** MMLU has ~14K items, GSM8K has ~8K. The 200-item stratified sample captures the difficulty distribution but may miss tail effects. The resampling stability test (Section 4.3) partially addresses this but only for MMLU.\n\n## 7. Strengths (bulleted)\n\n- **Cross-benchmark variance gradient is a genuine, well-supported empirical contribution** that unifies contradictory findings from prior single-benchmark studies. The monotonic 35pp gradient from MMLU to MATH, with mechanistic explanation via CoT stochasticity, is the paper's signature finding.\n\n- **Massive experimental scale**: 4M+ evaluation records, 5 benchmarks, 6 models, crossed factorial designs. Few empirical papers in this area match the breadth.\n\n- **D-study reproducibility budgets are immediately actionable**: specific item/prompt/seed allocations for G >= 0.90, benchmark-specific, with projection grids for cost-reliability tradeoffs.\n\n- **Engine confound discovery and correction** (vLLM V0 vs. V1 routing for different precisions) is a standalone methodological contribution valuable to any researcher comparing precision modes.\n\n- **Honest framing of the separability null**: infrastructure crossing framed as confirmatory rather than primary novelty, with nuanced decomposition into aggregate vs. item-conditional interactions.\n\n- **Comprehensive robustness checks**: item resampling stability, REML vs. Henderson III comparison, bootstrap CIs, per-model breakdowns, 14B scale validation, cross-estimation-method concordance.\n\n- **Bibliography substantially improved** over earlier drafts: 22 references covering variance sources, ANOVA decomposition, G-theory, and IRT literature.\n\n- **Counter-intuitive G pattern** (knowledge benchmarks yield lower G than reasoning benchmarks) is well-explained and adds insight beyond the headline gradient finding.\n\n## 8. Weaknesses (bulleted)\n\n- **Zero figures in a variance decomposition paper** is a serious presentation deficiency. Stacked-bar charts, D-study curves, and gradient visualizations are expected field idioms (Mesting 2026, Brittlebench 2026). 14 tables + 0 figures makes the paper dense and hard to scan.\n\n- **Infrastructure crossing is the claimed methodological advance over Mesting but yields near-null results** (< 0.3% direct interactions). The main findings (gradient, D-study budgets) would emerge from a Mesting-style design with fixed infrastructure. The paper is honest about this but it weakens the novelty argument.\n\n- **Title promises \"Evaluation Metrics\" but the paper delivers only binary scoring** in the main text. Logprob analysis is a one-paragraph appendix. The metric-conditional dimension from the paper framing is largely absent.\n\n- **No models above 14B.** Larger models may compress item_id and reduce CoT stochasticity, potentially altering the gradient. Generality claims should be scoped to 7-14B.\n\n- **Fine-tuning extension (RoBERTa-large, encoder-only, classification)** is architecturally distant from the decoder inference setting. Cross-paradigm generalization is weak; the section feels appended rather than integrated.\n\n- **Missing references**: GeneralizIT (2024), Kunievsky et al. (2025), Brittlebench (2026), Bui et al. (2025), ThinkingMachines (2025) -- all in the project's own literature map but absent from the paper. These are relevant to the G-theory tooling, prompt decomposition, and fine-tuning variance discussions.\n\n- **No cost analysis for recommended budgets.** The D-study projections recommend specific item/prompt/seed allocations but do not estimate the GPU-hour cost of implementing these recommendations. Without cost, the budgets are theoretical.\n\n- **200-item samples from much larger benchmarks.** Resampling stability is tested only for MMLU. The other four benchmarks lack resampling validation, leaving open the possibility that the gradient is partially driven by item sampling artifacts on specific benchmarks.\n\n## 9. Questions for Authors\n\n1. The cross-benchmark gradient is your strongest finding. Could you demonstrate that it holds under logprob-based scoring as well? The one-paragraph Appendix B suggests item_id drops to 42.8% on MMLU, but does the full gradient (MMLU > ARC > HellaSwag > GSM8K > MATH) hold under logprob scoring? If not, is the gradient an artifact of binary scoring thresholds?\n\n2. Your D-study recommends 420 items + 3 prompts + 2 seeds for MMLU (2,520 total evaluations). How does this compare to Polo et al.'s tinyBenchmarks recommendation for MMLU? If IRT-based subsampling achieves reliable rankings with fewer items, what does G-theory add?\n\n3. DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B shows elevated temperature x item (8.8%) and seed x item (5.2%) on MMLU -- above the 8B range (Table 13). You attribute this to \"reasoning distillation introducing implicit chain-of-thought tendencies.\" Can you verify this mechanistically? Does DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B produce longer or more variable outputs on MMLU items compared to the other models?\n\n4. The separability null holds for 2 GPU types (Section 6). How confident are you that this extends to, say, A100 vs. H100 vs. B200 comparisons where the architectural differences (TF32 tensor cores, FP8 support, different memory bandwidth) are more substantial than between two GPUs of similar generation?\n\n5. You fix the judge model, unlike Mesting who includes judge as a facet. Since Mesting found judge identity as the dominant source (43.8%), your entire variance structure sits \"underneath\" the judge variance. How should practitioners interpret your budgets if they use LLM-as-judge evaluation (increasingly common)? Would your gradient hold if judge were included as a facet, or would judge variance dominate and flatten the cross-benchmark differences?\n\n6. The fine-tuning G-study finds item_id at rank-1 across all tasks (35.8-43.8%). But Bui et al. (2025) report extreme seed variance (RTES variance = 18.22) for RoBERTa on RTE. How do your RTE fine-tuning results compare to Bui et al.? Are the magnitudes consistent?\n\n7. Current-practice G < 0.25 is your most alarming finding. Have you computed what fraction of published leaderboard rankings would change if evaluations were repeated with different seeds/prompts? A \"rank instability\" analysis (e.g., what percentage of model-pair orderings flip under re-evaluation) would make the inadequacy finding more concrete than an abstract G coefficient.\n\n8. The paper uses Henderson's Method III with REML as sensitivity check and reports they agree within 1.4pp. Have you considered Bayesian variance component estimation (e.g., via Stan or brms)? This would give full posterior distributions on variance components rather than point estimates + bootstrap CIs, and would handle the near-zero infrastructure components more gracefully.\n\n## 10. Minor Issues\n\n- Table 5 header says \"4 models pooled\" but the design description mentions \"4 models x 2 precisions x ...\" -- clarify whether model is pooled or crossed in this analysis.\n- Table 7 (5-benchmark gradient) shows G_full for GSM8K (0.55) higher than MATH (0.50), breaking the monotonic pattern. The text does not explain this reversal; add a brief note.\n- The abstract says \"over 4 million evaluation records\" but summing the reported record counts (1,036,800 + 1,382,400 + 460,800 + 691,200 + 345,600 + 172,800 + fine-tuning) exceeds 4M. Provide an exact total or say \"approximately.\"\n- Section 3.2 says \"2-3 levels\" for temperature but does not specify which benchmarks use 2 vs. 3 levels. Clarify.\n- Table 1 (MMLU variance): the \"infra x method\" row aggregates multiple interaction terms. Break this out or provide the individual terms in an appendix.\n- The paper references \"Section 7\" (comprehensive analysis) as Section \\ref{sec:comprehensive} but the numbering in the LaTeX may not match if figures are added later. Verify cross-references.\n- Appendix C on higher-order interactions is interesting (temp x seed x item = 1.8%) but could be expanded. Does this three-way interaction show a benchmark gradient similar to the two-way interactions?\n\n## 11. Score\n\n**Score: 4/6 (Weak Accept)**\n\nThis is a well-executed, large-scale empirical study that applies G-theory to multi-benchmark LLM evaluation and discovers a genuine empirical regularity (the cross-benchmark variance gradient). The D-study budgets are practically useful, the experimental scale is impressive, and the paper has improved substantially from earlier drafts -- the bibliography is now adequate, the framing is honest about confirmatory vs. novel contributions, and the robustness checks are thorough. The cross-benchmark gradient as a unifying explanation for contradictory prior findings is the paper's strongest contribution and clears the novelty bar for a top venue.\n\nThe paper is held back by: (a) zero figures, which is a serious presentation weakness for a variance decomposition paper; (b) the infrastructure crossing being the claimed advance over Mesting yet yielding near-null results; (c) missing metric-conditional analysis despite the title; and (d) scope limitations (7-14B only, no API models, encoder-only fine-tuning). The first issue (figures) is easily fixable and would likely move the paper to a solid accept. The others are structural but not disqualifying.\n\nThe balance tips toward accept because the cross-benchmark gradient is genuinely novel, the D-study budgets fill a real practical gap, and the \"G < 0.25 everywhere\" finding is an important wake-up call for the field. The paper would benefit from another revision addressing the figure deficit and the metric-conditional analysis, but the core contribution is sound.\n\n## 12. Confidence\n\n**Confidence: High**\n\nI am familiar with G-theory (Brennan 2001, Shavelson & Webb 1991), the LLM evaluation reproducibility literature (Sclar, Pezeshkpour, Yuan, Biderman, Mesting), and the IRT/benchmark efficiency literature (Polo, Rodriguez, Perlitz). I have reviewed the full paper text, the related work notes, and the paper framing documents. My main uncertainty is whether models above 14B would substantially alter the gradient pattern -- this is an empirical question the paper cannot answer with its current design.\n","numLines":219,"startLine":1,"totalLines":219}}}
